bare\n`three` and `@dimforge/rapier…` imports use the game's detected version, or the\nlatest version on the shelf when none is specified.\nUnsupported versions or missing package files are reported and their references\nare preserved. Games copied by hand must supply their own correct import map;\nuse `pnpm ingest` to apply the localization automatically.\n\nThe shelf includes all 13 Three.js releases from `0.180.0` through `0.186.1`\nand all 12 official Rapier variants at `0.19.3`, `0.20.0` and `0.21.0`: 2D/3D,\nstandard/SIMD/deterministic, each with ordinary and compatibility packages.\nSee `lib/vendor/README.md` for paths, import maps and verification commands.\n\n**Size.** GitHub refuses any single file over 100 MB and warns above 50 MB;\nthe scan flags both. Inputs or expanded packages over 95 MiB are reported and skipped. For the whole wall,\na library of ~400 games at ~2.5 MB each (~900 MB) packs to roughly 500 MB in\ngit, under GitHub's 1 GB recommendation and 2 GB push limit. Deploy it from\nthe repo: Vercel builds from GitHub with room to spare, whereas `vercel\ndeploy` from the folder is capped at 100 MB of uploads on the Hobby plan.\nPushes don't deploy, to keep Vercel's usage down (`\"git\": {\n\"deploymentEnabled\": false }` in `vercel.json`): to deploy, push with it set\nto `true`, then set it back to `false` in the next commit.\nReplaced builds stay in git history, so the repo grows with every update.\n\n## Running it locally\n\n```bash\npnpm dev          # http://localhost:7417\n```\n\nThere is no build step and no `node_modules` — `pnpm install` has nothing to\ninstall. The dev server re-scans `games/` on every refresh, so dropping a file\nin and hitting reload is the whole loop. It remembers what it read from each\n======VENDOR\nREADME.md\nextras\nmanifest.json\nrapier\nthree\n# Versioned browser libraries\n\nThis shelf lets imported games retain their requested engine version and\nrenderer. It is separate from the patched shared stack at `/lib/three/`,\n`/lib/rapier/` and `/lib/kit/`; existing games keep using those paths.\n\n## Available releases\n\nThree.js includes **every stable release and patch** in the requested range:\n\n`0.180.0`, `0.181.0`, `0.181.1`, `0.181.2`, `0.182.0`, `0.183.0`, `0.183.1`,\n`0.183.2`, `0.184.0`, `0.185.0`, `0.185.1`, `0.186.0`, `0.186.1`.\n\nEach is the full official npm package: classic WebGL and WebGPU builds, TSL,\nall addons, source modules, fonts, Draco and Basis decoders, and the license.\nThe versioned packages are upstream distributions, without the shared stack's\nlocal patches or WebGL-only exclusions.\n\nThe shelf also pins **Three.js 0.160.0** and older **Rapier 3D compat** releases:\n**0.14.0** for The Ash-Bound Prophecy and **0.19.0** for Ashbound: The Sleeper’s\nOath. These are full official packages, preserved on every sync so the games\nretain their original WebGL renderer and physics.\nThe r160 browser fixture exercises classic WebGL and addons; newer releases\nalso exercise the modern WebGPU build and TSL.\n\nRapier includes **0.19.3, 0.20.0 and 0.21.0** for all 12 official packages:\n\n| Dimension | Standard | SIMD | Deterministic |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2D | `rapier2d`, `rapier2d-compat` | `rapier2d-simd`, `rapier2d-simd-compat` | `rapier2d-deterministic`, `rapier2d-deterministic-compat` |\n| 3D | `rapier3d`, `rapier3d-compat` | `rapier3d-simd`, `rapier3d-simd-compat` | `rapier3d-deterministic`, `rapier3d-deterministic-compat` |\n\nAll package names have the `@dimforge/` scope. Stable releases are included;\nnightly/canary builds are outside this shelf. The starting version `0.193`\nwas interpreted as the published release `0.19.3`.\n\n## Importing games\n\nUse `pnpm ingest <file|folder|zip>`. The importer rewrites recognized Three.js\nand Rapier URLs in HTML, JavaScript and CSS to local paths. Supported hosts:\njsDelivr (npm and Three.js GitHub tags), unpkg, esm.sh, Skypack, JSPM, cdnjs,\nand the official Three.js build/examples paths.\n\nExact version pins stay exact. Partial versions and `^0.x`/`~0.x` ranges select\na compatible local patch. Unmapped bare imports use the detected package\nversion, or the latest version in `manifest.json` when none is specified;\nunversioned CDN references also use the latest. The importer adds per-version\nimport-map scopes so addons and TSL resolve their own version of Three.js.\nExisting mappings, game assets, embedded bundles and game logic are retained.\nThe dry-run report lists dependencies that would become local. Re-importing\nthe same input still skips it as a duplicate.\n\nUnavailable releases and files are reported and left unchanged. A URL for an\nold global `three.min.js` build is not silently replaced by an ES module:\nthose global builds are not part of r180 onward. Arbitrary missing game files,\nother libraries, custom CDN-generated bundles, or incompatible game code still\nneed individual attention. Installing engines cannot supply missing models,\ntextures, levels or audio.\n\n## Manual paths\n======SESSION-HUB-HEAD\n`node scripts/explore-browser.mjs`. Pass a deployed site URL to check production.\n\n## Playing together: AI Hub sessions\n\nEvery game can be played with up to three friends, each on their own device,\nthrough an **AI Hub session**: the hub deals the games (Random Mode), keeps\nthe clock, carries the network traffic and keeps score. There is no chat and\nno way for players to message each other; they talk in an app of their own.\n\nWhat a game must do is the **AI Hub Session Protocol**, specified in\n[`lib/hub/README.md`](lib/hub/README.md) and served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>. That page is the\nwhole interface: it is the one thing handed to whichever agent builds a game.\nThe page holds only the interface and the hub's facts: no example values,\nsuggested numbers or design advice, since an agent takes any of those as the\nway to build the game. A game declares one of three modes:\n\n- **Parallel**: everyone plays their own copy, from the same\n  start, on the same clock; the hub draws a live scoreboard from each copy's\n  progress. Every game with a score or a finish can do it, with no networking,\n  and it is how the existing library joins sessions.\n- **Ghost**: parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters while they are in the same area of the game, without them\n  touching its world.\n- **Shared world**: one world for everyone, run by the host's copy.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n\nTwo scripts keep thousands of games on the protocol without editing them by\nhand:\n\n```bash","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TrFHEoGcM9kF3dEdVqNW2o","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9NCZJcwnejT6M5vdKr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JQCRS1K8g57F5j2t7wRGQX","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211923__xa49253/work/.research/engine/my-3d2dge && wc -l docs/FEEDBACK-LOG.md docs/HANDOFF-REVIEW.md docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md docs/HANDOFF-COOP.md docs/STANDARD-FEATURES.md docs/MOCAP.md; sed -n 1,140p docs/FEEDBACK-LOG.md | cut -c1-420","description":"Read the engine's builder feedback log"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16116,"cache_read_input_tokens":119266,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16116},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ea1b61d-e1b8-4350-be76-ae4a4cbc51a9","uuid":"47a11ddf-381c-401f-ad6b-9a6511d4388f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:39.944Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9NCGSWtsyhW41CdbgM","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01JQCRS1K8g57F5j2t7wRGQX":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211923__xa49253/work/.research/engine/my-3d2dge && wc -l docs/FEEDBACK-LOG.md docs/HANDOFF-REVIEW.md docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md docs/HANDOFF-COOP.md docs/STANDARD-FEATURES.md docs/MOCAP.md; sed -n 1,140p docs/FEEDBACK-LOG.md | cut -c1-420","description":"Read the engine's builder feedback log"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JQCRS1K8g57F5j2t7wRGQX","type":"tool_result","content":"   360 docs/FEEDBACK-LOG.md\n    51 docs/HANDOFF-REVIEW.md\n   116 docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md\n  1117 docs/HANDOFF-COOP.md\n   127 docs/STANDARD-FEATURES.md\n   402 docs/MOCAP.md\n  2173 total\n# Feedback log\n\nThis file keeps the points we did **not** act on from models (and people) who built games on the engine. The same point\ncoming back from later sources is how a pattern shows, so it can be weighed again. What we do act on goes into a plan\n(`docs/HANDOFF-*.md`) and then into the changelog.\n\n- **Sources.** Each round of feedback is a source, numbered `F1`, `F2`, ..., newest last. Each point not acted on gets one\n  line (`F1.1`, ...) with a theme.\n- **Status.** Each point is one of three:\n  - **no**: not a problem for what the engine is for;\n  - **deferred**: valid, but not now, with when to take it up;\n  - **watch**: handled indirectly, or too early to tell.\n- **Patterns.** The themes table counts sources. A theme reaches its threshold at three sources, or at two from different\n  model families. Then the next plan takes it up and decides again. When that plan is done, the theme says **taken up**\n  and its row and points say in which version; a later source on it counts afresh.\n- **Never cleaned up**, like `docs/DECISIONS.md`. A point taken up later is marked **Taken up in v0.x.y** and kept.\n\n## Themes\n\n| Theme | Status | Sources | Take it up when |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Passages under roofs and bridges (one column per cell) | deferred | F1 | a second source asks; the stealth starter needs a bridge it can't fake |\n| Side-scroller and shooter kits unused by modern third-person games | no | F1 | if third-person games keep skipping them, give their package a leaner manual |\n| Melee helpers (`E.Attack`, `E.Combo`) bypassed | watch | F1 | a game built after the height-aware queries and actions still bypasses them |\n| GPU lighting unused | taken up | F1, F6 | reached: two model families (F6.5: no lighting of any kind). The next plan (`docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`, v0.51.5; folded in v0.59.1, `git show 4746448:docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`) takes it up: rethink its space in the manuals. **Taken up in v0.54.0**: lighting is the Look target's step 9 and the hand-back checklist's; canvas lighting leads, GPU lighting falls back to it by itself; the\n| One fixed custom view, views never switched | watch | F1 | three sources: make one view the default for third-person kits and their checks |\n| Reading the engine's source (F1: its size) | taken up | F1, F2, F3, F4, F6; since v0.53.0: F7 | reached: two model families. The next plan (`docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`, v0.51.5; folded in v0.59.1, `git show 4746448:docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`) takes it up: F2.1, F3.1 and F4.1 list what the index and cards lack. `docs/HANDOFF-ASSETS.md` (v0.25.1) takes up the asset names (props, textures, presets) and four garbled index lines;\n| The look below the PS1/N64 bar | taken up | F2, F3, F4, F6; since v0.59.0: F7 | reached: three games at 6/10. The next plan (`docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`, v0.51.5; folded in v0.59.1, `git show 4746448:docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`) takes it up (F2.2, F3.2, F4.2). `docs/HANDOFF-ASSETS.md` (v0.25.1) takes up objects, foliage, materials and contact shadows (done in v0.35.0 to v0.38.0, the plan folded into `docs/ASSETS.md` and `docs\n| `E.Shell` unused, the interface drawn on the canvas | taken up | F2, F3, F4, F6 | reached: three games with modern interfaces skipped it. The next plan (`docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`, v0.51.5; folded in v0.59.1, `git show 4746448:docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`) takes it up (F4.3). **Taken up in v0.55.0**: the STEALTH slice's and the template game's HUDs are `E.Shell`'s HTML (the objective with its distance, health, status, a taked\n| Climbing any wall and gliding written by the game (`E.Mover` climbs ledges) | taken up | F3, F4, F6 | reached: three games wrote their own (F6.4). The next plan (`docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`, v0.51.5; folded in v0.59.1, `git show 4746448:docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`) takes it up. **Taken up in v0.58.0**: `E.Mover` climbs any wall, opt-in (`climbAny`: take hold on the ground or in the air, climb up and along as the screen shows \n| Not verified on physical phones or native GPUs | no | F1, F8 | a game heads to production (principle 10) |\n| Saves, checkpoints and objectives written by the game | watch | F1 | three sources write the same checkpoint or objective code |\n| Music written by the game for stealth and chases | watch | F1 | two more sources: built-in `stealth` and `chase` songs |\n| Production hardening asked of every game (PWA, wake lock, folds, physical devices, other gamepad mappings) | deferred | F5 | a game heads to production (principle 10) |\n| Settings nothing honors yet (frame-rate limit, brightness, text size, HUD scale) | deferred | F5 | the engine or a game has something they would change |\n| Assists each game writes (aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle, captions) | watch | F5 | two builders write the same assist: the engine gets it, as a standard setting |\n| A dialogue piece in `E.Shell` (talking characters are `E.Dialog` on the canvas) | watch | F4 | a source built after v0.55.0 still draws its interface on the canvas, or its prompts collide with the dialogue (F4.3) |\n| A turned three-quarter view (yaw 0: pillars lay their tops on the wall behind) | watch | F4 | a source after v0.57.0 still sees three-quarter walls and pillars as flat planes (F4.2): a small yaw is the owner's call |\n| Pitched roofs for `TileMap` types (the stealth starter's belfry draws one with `r.queue`) | deferred | none yet | a source asks for roofs |\n| GPU lighting in headless checks (WebGPU in `tools/check.mjs`'s browser) | deferred | F1 | a game leads with GPU lighting and its checks need to see it; canvas lighting shows in every check (D45) |\n| The look's remainder: small figures that merge when they overlap (F4.2), empty fields (F6.2) | watch | F7 (F7.2: the kart's empty fields, the tactics map's overlapping units; before it, none: the parts of F4.2 and F6.2 the next plan left; they predate it, so they don't count afresh; F2.2's foliage and F3.2's pines and thin shadows predate the asset store's foliage and contact shadows, v0.35.0 to v0.38.0, and F6 did\n| What the cards point to, not in the handoff package (the genres' slices, the 3D engine) | taken up | F7 | the next change to the handoff package: it carries the slices and the 3D engine, or its cards say where the kits are (F7.3). **Taken up in v0.85.0**: the package carries every 2D kit's game readable (`kits/`: a page that plays each, their table, a headless test), its README starts an agent from its genre's kit,\n| A genre module the card names, unused (the game writes its own) | watch | F7 | a second source writes what its genre's module does (F7.4); since v0.85.0 the handoff package carries the kits, each built on its genre's modules (D73) |\n| The benchmark's own measures (what counts as drawn, the views the check shows, one bar for every brief) | deferred | F7 | the next change to the benchmark (F7.5) |\n| A headless game that differs from the page (`game.go` switching at once in tests) | no | F8 | never: the same inputs give the same state with any renderer or none (principle 5); tests advance past the fade or set `game.fadeTime = 0` |\n| Maps beyond a height field (voxels and floors over floors, hex floors with picking) | deferred | F8 | a second source asks; the grid module's hex grids and 3D's levels meanwhile |\n| Rigs beyond the Humanoid and the Blob in the core (four legs, wings, serpents) | deferred | F8 | a second source asks; Emberdeep's crawlers, serpent and Watcher are its own |\n| The 3D engine's level as data that changes in play, a headless stand-in for it, a crawler's cells | deferred | F8 | a second source asks, or the CRAWLER kit needs a secret wall |\n| Cinematic cameras (shots along a rail with line of sight) | deferred | F8 | a second source asks |\n| Perception's tuning (suspicion that never decays while seen) | watch | F8 | a second stealth game tunes it by hand |\n| The pseudo-3D road square by default (one focal length) | deferred | F8 | the owner picks the ROAD kit's look with a retuned camera; `square: true` meanwhile |\n\n## Sources\n\n### F1 · 2026-10-10 · Vesper: The Ninth Bell (Assassin's Creed)\n\n- **Builder:** GPT-6 Astra Pro (OpenAI), in a ChatGPT cloud workspace with a shell, Node 24 and headless Chromium 131;\n  no web research.\n- **Handed:** the full engine as one file, `my-3d2dge.js` v0.17.2 (`dev-build`), and nothing else.\n- **Built:** an isometric stealth and parkour mission in one offline HTML file. That is about 50k cl100k tokens of game\n  code (30% UI, 16% facades, 16% traversal, sight, guard AI and takedowns), an engine patch (`vesper-1`: the clock,\n  flow-field revisions, the FPS counter) and 16 simulation checks.\n- **Used:** the renderer, views, `TileMap`, `Body`, `FlowField`, `Humanoid`, input, camera, particles, audio, the font\n  and storage.\n- **Acted on:** the plan `docs/HANDOFF-VESPER-FEEDBACK.md` (deleted when done; `git show\n  8b736f5:docs/HANDOFF-VESPER-FEEDBACK.md`), in six releases: the clock and the caches (v0.17.5, D16), one height-aware\n  spatial API (v0.18.0), the handoff package with the API index and contract tests (v0.19.0, D17), a third-person kit\n  (v0.20.0, D18), an HTML UI shell (v0.21.0) and the benchmark (v0.22.0, D19).\n- **Where its output went** (about 50k cl100k tokens of game code, the engine excluded; the benchmark's baseline):\n  UI 15.2k, 30% (HTML 2.8k, CSS 7.7k, glue 1.5k, HUD and minimap 2.2k, results 1.0k: `E.Shell` now, which rebuilds it in\n  2.1k); facades and cached detail drawing 7.9k, 16% (the decoration hook and facade options); traversal, guard AI,\n  takedowns, perception and markers 7.8k, 16% (the spatial queries, `E.Mover`, `E.ACTIONS`, perception); city layout,\n  dressing and floors 6.6k, 13% (the game's content); the simulation core 4.6k, 9% (the game's rules); setup, input,\n  rigs and drawing 4.1k, 8% (the `THIRDPERSON` preset and the stealth starter); saves and checkpoints 1.9k, 4% (F1.8);\n  music and sound 1.8k, 4% (F1.9). It also ran its own 1/60 s substeps inside the engine's update, not trusting the clock.\n- **Plan items not done:**\n  - The traversal clip set (Phase 3, item 2: cut from the motion store where code couldn't match a clip): not needed,\n    the rig's code states met the bar on filmstrips. The store has them if a game wants captured motion: `ClimbUp_1m_RM`,\n    Climb Wall, Ledge Hang, Pipe Climb, Crawl, `Walk_Stealth`, `Run_Stealth`, `Crouch_Walk`, Roll, `Dive_Roll`,\n    `Land_Three_Point` and the NinjaJump pair (the Quaternius, Mesh2Motion and CMU catalogs).\n  - The benchmark's runs (Phase 5): the tool is built; the runs spend money and wait on the owner (D19). **Taken up in\n    v0.22.1 and v0.22.2:** the owner said yes, and all three briefs ran (F2, F3, F4; `DOCTRINE.md`'s measures).\n- **Not acted on:**\n  - **F1.1** · kits unused · \"PlatformMap, Platformer, Blob, bullets, and projectile patterns: these systems did not\n    fit the required movement or actors.\" **No:** they are for other genres.\n  - **F1.2** · melee helpers bypassed · \"Combat needed explicit rules for height, perception, and takedowns.\"\n    **Watch:** the plan fixes the cause (height-aware tests, actions, perception) and leaves the helpers as they are.\n  - **F1.3** · GPU lighting unused · \"A single Canvas view kept rendering and verification consistent.\" **Watch.**\n    **Taken up in v0.54.0.**\n  - **F1.4** · one fixed custom view, for the same reason. It made its own view (yaw 45, pitch 35, scale 1.27) and\n    never switched. **Watch.**\n  - **F1.5** · passages under things · \"The single-column map model also prevents walkable passages beneath roofs or\n    bridges.\" **Deferred.** The likely shape, when it is taken up, is one solid span per cell: a type gets `base`, so it\n    is solid from `base` to `h` instead of from 0. Then collision lets a body pass under when its top is below `base`;\n    `groundAt` picks the floor or the deck by the body's `z`; `ray` passes under; `FlowField` gets two nodes in such\n    cells; the wall baker draws the span and its underside; and draw order uses footprints (v0.20.0). Spike it first: a\n    bridge over a canal and an arch over a street, walked over, walked under and seen through.\n  - **F1.6** · file size · \"The large source file and references to companion tools that were not supplied increased\n    inspection work.\" **No:** reading the source was a symptom of the missing docs and tools, which the plan\n    supplies. Input tokens are cheap, and one file is how the engine ships (D5).\n  - **F1.7** · device performance · \"They do not establish performance on physical phones or native GPUs.\" **No:**\n    principle 10, discovery first.\n  - **F1.8** · game-side systems · it added objective and checkpoint-validation modules (1.9k tokens for saves).\n    **Watch:** that is game logic until several games write the same thing.\n  - **F1.9** · music · seen in its code, not said: it wrote its own quiet, pursuit and win songs (1.8k tokens with its\n    sound effects). **Watch.**\n\n### F2 · 2026-10-11 · the benchmark's Assassin's Creed run (\"The Acre Memory\")\n\n- **Builder:** Claude Code (Opus 5.5) with `--safe-mode` (none of this machine's instructions or tools), run by\n  `tools/agent-bench.mjs`; its brief was Vesper's (F1), minimal.\n- **Handed:** the handoff package v0.22.0 (`dist/handoff/`) in an empty directory.\n- **Built:** an isometric Acre of sandstone roofs: climbing, guards with sight, takedowns, portrait dialogue, a minimap,\n  a title. About 27.5k tokens of game code and a 4.3k-token headless test, in 39 minutes and 185.6k output tokens\n  (thinking included); its first build passes `tools/check.mjs` and its test. The subscription's usage limit cut it off in\n  its last read-through. It wrote its own headless playthrough bot (a `FlowField` picking the way, input only) to test\n  the whole mission. It didn't patch the engine. The judge: 6/10, \"strong SNES-era work rather than PS1 polish\".\n- **Not acted on (yet):**\n  - **F2.1** · reading the source · it read the engine below its header 18 times (13 by v0.22.2's stricter count, which\n    leaves out scripts that only load the engine), for: the third-person kit's own\n    sections (17b-17d) rather than their index lines; the rig's state keys (`flip`, `hang`: the index lists rig methods\n    and fields, not what `rig.update` takes); the backdrop and prop preset names and the backdrop's `sun`; `emissive`;\n    `px.blend`; the song format (`steps`, `bpm`); `E.Combo`, `E.Attack` and `game.hitFx`; how `Dialog` holds play;\n    hats and hoods; the actor box's size. **Threshold** (with F1): the next plan takes it up, likely as index entries\n    for option and state keys, preset lists and the song format. **Taken up in v0.53.0.**\n  - **F2.2** · the look · flat lighting, unshaded foliage, and the hero drawn as cyan x-ray dither while he stands in a\n    rooftop recess (the stealth starter's `xray` default). **Watch.**\n    **Taken up in v0.54.0 (the lighting) and v0.56.0 (the x-ray).**\n  - **F2.3** · `E.Shell` unused · it drew its interface on the canvas (`Dialog` with portraits, `E.ui` boxes). The brief\n    didn't ask for HTML. **Watch.** **Taken up in v0.55.0.**\n\n### F3 · 2026-10-11 · the benchmark's Breath of the Wild run (\"Wild Heights\")\n\n- **Builder:** Claude Code (Opus 5.5) with `--safe-mode`, run by `tools/agent-bench.mjs`; the minimal Breath of the\n  Wild village brief.\n- **Handed:** the handoff package v0.22.1 in an empty directory.\n- **Built:** a Kakariko-style village in iso and three-quarter views: climb any wall on a stamina wheel, sprint, swim,\n  a paraglider with a bonfire updraft, four Spirit Orbs that each teach one move, a second wheel from the statue, a\n  tower to wake, an ending with stats; talking villagers with portraits. About 27.9k tokens of game code and a 4.3k-token\n  headless test (48 checks), in 38 minutes, 209k output tokens and $12.75; its first build passes the check and its","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ea1b61d-e1b8-4350-be76-ae4a4cbc51a9","uuid":"77eb9f3f-f3f7-4805-9a4b-b3932aafaf50","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:39.974Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"   360 docs/FEEDBACK-LOG.md\n    51 docs/HANDOFF-REVIEW.md\n   116 docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md\n  1117 docs/HANDOFF-COOP.md\n   127 docs/STANDARD-FEATURES.md\n   402 docs/MOCAP.md\n  2173 total\n# Feedback log\n\nThis file keeps the points we did **not** act on from models (and people) who built games on the engine. The same point\ncoming back from later sources is how a pattern shows, so it can be weighed again. What we do act on goes into a plan\n(`docs/HANDOFF-*.md`) and then into the changelog.\n\n- **Sources.** Each round of feedback is a source, numbered `F1`, `F2`, ..., newest last. Each point not acted on gets one\n  line (`F1.1`, ...) with a theme.\n- **Status.** Each point is one of three:\n  - **no**: not a problem for what the engine is for;\n  - **deferred**: valid, but not now, with when to take it up;\n  - **watch**: handled indirectly, or too early to tell.\n- **Patterns.** The themes table counts sources. A theme reaches its threshold at three sources, or at two from different\n  model families. Then the next plan takes it up and decides again. When that plan is done, the theme says **taken up**\n  and its row and points say in which version; a later source on it counts afresh.\n- **Never cleaned up**, like `docs/DECISIONS.md`. A point taken up later is marked **Taken up in v0.x.y** and kept.\n\n## Themes\n\n| Theme | Status | Sources | Take it up when |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Passages under roofs and bridges (one column per cell) | deferred | F1 | a second source asks; the stealth starter needs a bridge it can't fake |\n| Side-scroller and shooter kits unused by modern third-person games | no | F1 | if third-person games keep skipping them, give their package a leaner manual |\n| Melee helpers (`E.Attack`, `E.Combo`) bypassed | watch | F1 | a game built after the height-aware queries and actions still bypasses them |\n| GPU lighting unused | taken up | F1, F6 | reached: two model families (F6.5: no lighting of any kind). The next plan (`docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`, v0.51.5; folded in v0.59.1, `git show 4746448:docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`) takes it up: rethink its space in the manuals. **Taken up in v0.54.0**: lighting is the Look target's step 9 and the hand-back checklist's; canvas lighting leads, GPU lighting falls back to it by itself; the\n| One fixed custom view, views never switched | watch | F1 | three sources: make one view the default for third-person kits and their checks |\n| Reading the engine's source (F1: its size) | taken up | F1, F2, F3, F4, F6; since v0.53.0: F7 | reached: two model families. The next plan (`docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`, v0.51.5; folded in v0.59.1, `git show 4746448:docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`) takes it up: F2.1, F3.1 and F4.1 list what the index and cards lack. `docs/HANDOFF-ASSETS.md` (v0.25.1) takes up the asset names (props, textures, presets) and four garbled index lines;\n| The look below the PS1/N64 bar | taken up | F2, F3, F4, F6; since v0.59.0: F7 | reached: three games at 6/10. The next plan (`docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`, v0.51.5; folded in v0.59.1, `git show 4746448:docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`) takes it up (F2.2, F3.2, F4.2). `docs/HANDOFF-ASSETS.md` (v0.25.1) takes up objects, foliage, materials and contact shadows (done in v0.35.0 to v0.38.0, the plan folded into `docs/ASSETS.md` and `docs\n| `E.Shell` unused, the interface drawn on the canvas | taken up | F2, F3, F4, F6 | reached: three games with modern interfaces skipped it. The next plan (`docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`, v0.51.5; folded in v0.59.1, `git show 4746448:docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`) takes it up (F4.3). **Taken up in v0.55.0**: the STEALTH slice's and the template game's HUDs are `E.Shell`'s HTML (the objective with its distance, health, status, a taked\n| Climbing any wall and gliding written by the game (`E.Mover` climbs ledges) | taken up | F3, F4, F6 | reached: three games wrote their own (F6.4). The next plan (`docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`, v0.51.5; folded in v0.59.1, `git show 4746448:docs/HANDOFF-NEXT.md`) takes it up. **Taken up in v0.58.0**: `E.Mover` climbs any wall, opt-in (`climbAny`: take hold on the ground or in the air, climb up and along as the screen shows \n| Not verified on physical phones or native GPUs | no | F1, F8 | a game heads to production (principle 10) |\n| Saves, checkpoints and objectives written by the game | watch | F1 | three sources write the same checkpoint or objective code |\n| Music written by the game for stealth and chases | watch | F1 | two more sources: built-in `stealth` and `chase` songs |\n| Production hardening asked of every game (PWA, wake lock, folds, physical devices, other gamepad mappings) | deferred | F5 | a game heads to production (principle 10) |\n| Settings nothing honors yet (frame-rate limit, brightness, text size, HUD scale) | deferred | F5 | the engine or a game has something they would change |\n| Assists each game writes (aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle, captions) | watch | F5 | two builders write the same assist: the engine gets it, as a standard setting |\n| A dialogue piece in `E.Shell` (talking characters are `E.Dialog` on the canvas) | watch | F4 | a source built after v0.55.0 still draws its interface on the canvas, or its prompts collide with the dialogue (F4.3) |\n| A turned three-quarter view (yaw 0: pillars lay their tops on the wall behind) | watch | F4 | a source after v0.57.0 still sees three-quarter walls and pillars as flat planes (F4.2): a small yaw is the owner's call |\n| Pitched roofs for `TileMap` types (the stealth starter's belfry draws one with `r.queue`) | deferred | none yet | a source asks for roofs |\n| GPU lighting in headless checks (WebGPU in `tools/check.mjs`'s browser) | deferred | F1 | a game leads with GPU lighting and its checks need to see it; canvas lighting shows in every check (D45) |\n| The look's remainder: small figures that merge when they overlap (F4.2), empty fields (F6.2) | watch | F7 (F7.2: the kart's empty fields, the tactics map's overlapping units; before it, none: the parts of F4.2 and F6.2 the next plan left; they predate it, so they don't count afresh; F2.2's foliage and F3.2's pines and thin shadows predate the asset store's foliage and contact shadows, v0.35.0 to v0.38.0, and F6 did\n| What the cards point to, not in the handoff package (the genres' slices, the 3D engine) | taken up | F7 | the next change to the handoff package: it carries the slices and the 3D engine, or its cards say where the kits are (F7.3). **Taken up in v0.85.0**: the package carries every 2D kit's game readable (`kits/`: a page that plays each, their table, a headless test), its README starts an agent from its genre's kit,\n| A genre module the card names, unused (the game writes its own) | watch | F7 | a second source writes what its genre's module does (F7.4); since v0.85.0 the handoff package carries the kits, each built on its genre's modules (D73) |\n| The benchmark's own measures (what counts as drawn, the views the check shows, one bar for every brief) | deferred | F7 | the next change to the benchmark (F7.5) |\n| A headless game that differs from the page (`game.go` switching at once in tests) | no | F8 | never: the same inputs give the same state with any renderer or none (principle 5); tests advance past the fade or set `game.fadeTime = 0` |\n| Maps beyond a height field (voxels and floors over floors, hex floors with picking) | deferred | F8 | a second source asks; the grid module's hex grids and 3D's levels meanwhile |\n| Rigs beyond the Humanoid and the Blob in the core (four legs, wings, serpents) | deferred | F8 | a second source asks; Emberdeep's crawlers, serpent and Watcher are its own |\n| The 3D engine's level as data that changes in play, a headless stand-in for it, a crawler's cells | deferred | F8 | a second source asks, or the CRAWLER kit needs a secret wall |\n| Cinematic cameras (shots along a rail with line of sight) | deferred | F8 | a second source asks |\n| Perception's tuning (suspicion that never decays while seen) | watch | F8 | a second stealth game tunes it by hand |\n| The pseudo-3D road square by default (one focal length) | deferred | F8 | the owner picks the ROAD kit's look with a retuned camera; `square: true` meanwhile |\n\n## Sources\n\n### F1 · 2026-10-10 · Vesper: The Ninth Bell (Assassin's Creed)\n\n- **Builder:** GPT-6 Astra Pro (OpenAI), in a ChatGPT cloud workspace with a shell, Node 24 and headless Chromium 131;\n  no web research.\n- **Handed:** the full engine as one file, `my-3d2dge.js` v0.17.2 (`dev-build`), and nothing else.\n- **Built:** an isometric stealth and parkour mission in one offline HTML file. That is about 50k cl100k tokens of game\n  code (30% UI, 16% facades, 16% traversal, sight, guard AI and takedowns), an engine patch (`vesper-1`: the clock,\n  flow-field revisions, the FPS counter) and 16 simulation checks.\n- **Used:** the renderer, views, `TileMap`, `Body`, `FlowField`, `Humanoid`, input, camera, particles, audio, the font\n  and storage.\n- **Acted on:** the plan `docs/HANDOFF-VESPER-FEEDBACK.md` (deleted when done; `git show\n  8b736f5:docs/HANDOFF-VESPER-FEEDBACK.md`), in six releases: the clock and the caches (v0.17.5, D16), one height-aware\n  spatial API (v0.18.0), the handoff package with the API index and contract tests (v0.19.0, D17), a third-person kit\n  (v0.20.0, D18), an HTML UI shell (v0.21.0) and the benchmark (v0.22.0, D19).\n- **Where its output went** (about 50k cl100k tokens of game code, the engine excluded; the benchmark's baseline):\n  UI 15.2k, 30% (HTML 2.8k, CSS 7.7k, glue 1.5k, HUD and minimap 2.2k, results 1.0k: `E.Shell` now, which rebuilds it in\n  2.1k); facades and cached detail drawing 7.9k, 16% (the decoration hook and facade options); traversal, guard AI,\n  takedowns, perception and markers 7.8k, 16% (the spatial queries, `E.Mover`, `E.ACTIONS`, perception); city layout,\n  dressing and floors 6.6k, 13% (the game's content); the simulation core 4.6k, 9% (the game's rules); setup, input,\n  rigs and drawing 4.1k, 8% (the `THIRDPERSON` preset and the stealth starter); saves and checkpoints 1.9k, 4% (F1.8);\n  music and sound 1.8k, 4% (F1.9). It also ran its own 1/60 s substeps inside the engine's update, not trusting the clock.\n- **Plan items not done:**\n  - The traversal clip set (Phase 3, item 2: cut from the motion store where code couldn't match a clip): not needed,\n    the rig's code states met the bar on filmstrips. The store has them if a game wants captured motion: `ClimbUp_1m_RM`,\n    Climb Wall, Ledge Hang, Pipe Climb, Crawl, `Walk_Stealth`, `Run_Stealth`, `Crouch_Walk`, Roll, `Dive_Roll`,\n    `Land_Three_Point` and the NinjaJump pair (the Quaternius, Mesh2Motion and CMU catalogs).\n  - The benchmark's runs (Phase 5): the tool is built; the runs spend money and wait on the owner (D19). **Taken up in\n    v0.22.1 and v0.22.2:** the owner said yes, and all three briefs ran (F2, F3, F4; `DOCTRINE.md`'s measures).\n- **Not acted on:**\n  - **F1.1** · kits unused · \"PlatformMap, Platformer, Blob, bullets, and projectile patterns: these systems did not\n    fit the required movement or actors.\" **No:** they are for other genres.\n  - **F1.2** · melee helpers bypassed · \"Combat needed explicit rules for height, perception, and takedowns.\"\n    **Watch:** the plan fixes the cause (height-aware tests, actions, perception) and leaves the helpers as they are.\n  - **F1.3** · GPU lighting unused · \"A single Canvas view kept rendering and verification consistent.\" **Watch.**\n    **Taken up in v0.54.0.**\n  - **F1.4** · one fixed custom view, for the same reason. It made its own view (yaw 45, pitch 35, scale 1.27) and\n    never switched. **Watch.**\n  - **F1.5** · passages under things · \"The single-column map model also prevents walkable passages beneath roofs or\n    bridges.\" **Deferred.** The likely shape, when it is taken up, is one solid span per cell: a type gets `base`, so it\n    is solid from `base` to `h` instead of from 0. Then collision lets a body pass under when its top is below `base`;\n    `groundAt` picks the floor or the deck by the body's `z`; `ray` passes under; `FlowField` gets two nodes in such\n    cells; the wall baker draws the span and its underside; and draw order uses footprints (v0.20.0). Spike it first: a\n    bridge over a canal and an arch over a street, walked over, walked under and seen through.\n  - **F1.6** · file size · \"The large source file and references to companion tools that were not supplied increased\n    inspection work.\" **No:** reading the source was a symptom of the missing docs and tools, which the plan\n    supplies. Input tokens are cheap, and one file is how the engine ships (D5).\n  - **F1.7** · device performance · \"They do not establish performance on physical phones or native GPUs.\" **No:**\n    principle 10, discovery first.\n  - **F1.8** · game-side systems · it added objective and checkpoint-validation modules (1.9k tokens for saves).\n    **Watch:** that is game logic until several games write the same thing.\n  - **F1.9** · music · seen in its code, not said: it wrote its own quiet, pursuit and win songs (1.8k tokens with its\n    sound effects). **Watch.**\n\n### F2 · 2026-10-11 · the benchmark's Assassin's Creed run (\"The Acre Memory\")\n\n- **Builder:** Claude Code (Opus 5.5) with `--safe-mode` (none of this machine's instructions or tools), run by\n  `tools/agent-bench.mjs`; its brief was Vesper's (F1), minimal.\n- **Handed:** the handoff package v0.22.0 (`dist/handoff/`) in an empty directory.\n- **Built:** an isometric Acre of sandstone roofs: climbing, guards with sight, takedowns, portrait dialogue, a minimap,\n  a title. About 27.5k tokens of game code and a 4.3k-token headless test, in 39 minutes and 185.6k output tokens\n  (thinking included); its first build passes `tools/check.mjs` and its test. The subscription's usage limit cut it off in\n  its last read-through. It wrote its own headless playthrough bot (a `FlowField` picking the way, input only) to test\n  the whole mission. It didn't patch the engine. The judge: 6/10, \"strong SNES-era work rather than PS1 polish\".\n- **Not acted on (yet):**\n  - **F2.1** · reading the source · it read the engine below its header 18 times (13 by v0.22.2's stricter count, which\n    leaves out scripts that only load the engine), for: the third-person kit's own\n    sections (17b-17d) rather than their index lines; the rig's state keys (`flip`, `hang`: the index lists rig methods\n    and fields, not what `rig.update` takes); the backdrop and prop preset names and the backdrop's `sun`; `emissive`;\n    `px.blend`; the song format (`steps`, `bpm`); `E.Combo`, `E.Attack` and `game.hitFx`; how `Dialog` holds play;\n    hats and hoods; the actor box's size. **Threshold** (with F1): the next plan takes it up, likely as index entries\n    for option and state keys, preset lists and the song format. **Taken up in v0.53.0.**\n  - **F2.2** · the look · flat lighting, unshaded foliage, and the hero drawn as cyan x-ray dither while he stands in a\n    rooftop recess (the stealth starter's `xray` default). **Watch.**\n    **Taken up in v0.54.0 (the lighting) and v0.56.0 (the x-ray).**\n  - **F2.3** · `E.Shell` unused · it drew its interface on the canvas (`Dialog` with portraits, `E.ui` boxes). The brief\n    didn't ask for HTML. **Watch.** **Taken up in v0.55.0.**\n\n### F3 · 2026-10-11 · the benchmark's Breath of the Wild run (\"Wild Heights\")\n\n- **Builder:** Claude Code (Opus 5.5) with `--safe-mode`, run by `tools/agent-bench.mjs`; the minimal Breath of the\n  Wild village brief.\n- **Handed:** the handoff package v0.22.1 in an empty directory.\n- **Built:** a Kakariko-style village in iso and three-quarter views: climb any wall on a stamina wheel, sprint, swim,\n  a paraglider with a bonfire updraft, four Spirit Orbs that each teach one move, a second wheel from the statue, a\n  tower to wake, an ending with stats; talking villagers with portraits. About 27.9k tokens of game code and a 4.3k-token\n  headless test (48 checks), in 38 minutes, 209k output tokens and $12.75; its first build passes the check and its","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JQCRS1K8g57F5j2t7wRGQX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
