{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017P4CzdBq9EhiDqqWsPv5rc","type":"tool_result","content":"# Handoff: co-op (up to four players, each free to go anywhere)\n\n> **Status: TBD, on hold (2026-10-16).** The owner is thinking it over. Nothing here is to be built until they say\n> go and answer section 13.\n>\n> **Since it was written (2026-10-18; checked again 2026-10-21 at v0.59.1):** v0.47.0 to v0.51.1 went to the 3D shooter\n> kit, and v0.51.2 to v0.59.1 emptied Temporary (D44), made the engine tier's private names public (v0.52.0: the\n> `_lastView` of section 4's row 4 is `rig.lastView`) and carried out the next plan (lighting, `E.Shell` HUDs, the x-ray,\n> walls, `E.Mover`'s climbing and gliding; D45 to D48). So each phase takes the next minor version when it merges, not\n> the number it was first given; check the facts it relies on against the code of the day (section 17's prompt says\n> how). The budgets below are as of v0.59.1.\n\nThe plan for co-op multiplayer in Emberdeep: up to four players in one game, each going wherever they like, against\nmonsters whose life and damage grow with the players around them. It was made on 2026-10-16 against v0.46.1\n(`cfa866a`). It draws on five audits of the game and the engine (quoted here by file and line) and on three\nmeasurements (section 2.3). It is for the agent that implements it, and for the owner, who answers section 13. It is\ntemporary: when the work lands, move what lasts into `docs/COOP.md`, `src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md` and `docs/DECISIONS.md`,\nthen delete this file, as v0.38.1 and v0.43.3 did with finished plans.\n\n**The brief (from the owner):**\n- Co-op for up to four players. It should be essentially the same single-player experience.\n- No extra monsters spawn. Instead, enemy life and damage rise with the number of players.\n- Do whatever the server, client and host sides need so that it feels lag free.\n- No anti-cheat.\n- Any player can go wherever they want in the game, as long as they can get there. Nobody is tied to another player\n  or has to wait for one: \"a super mini-MMO\".\n- No voice or text communication. Emotes and emote animations, standard pings and map pings are in.\n\n**The plan in brief:**\n1. **Every place is a world of its own, shared by the players in it.** The places are each depth and the Proving\n   Grounds. The players in a place all simulate it in deterministic lockstep: the same seed and the same inputs each\n   tick give the same world on every screen. This is the engine's own invariant (principle 5) put to work. Every\n   mechanic, monster, boss, effect and power then works in co-op as it is written, with no network version of any of\n   them.\n2. **Emberhold is the exception.** Each player runs their own town, and the others appear in it driven by their\n   inputs (section 3.1). Nothing in town needs to be agreed, and a town may run for hours.\n3. **Nobody waits for anybody outside their place.** Places run independently. A player alone in a place runs it with\n   no delay at all, which is single player exactly.\n4. **In a shared place, inputs take effect a few ticks late, to cover the network.** The estimates in section 7.1:\n   about 17 ms on the same Wi-Fi, 33 ms across a city and 50 to 70 ms across a country or on a phone network.\n   - Walking and turning show at once: the hero is drawn ahead of the world (the lead, section 7.3).\n   - Skills, dodges and monsters' reactions arrive on the delay.\n   - Phase 0 lets the owner feel this on their phone before anything else is built.\n5. **There is no game server.** Players connect directly (WebRTC data channels), at a few kilobytes a second between\n   any two of them (section 3.5).\n   - A small relay introduces them and carries traffic for the few who can't connect directly. It runs no game.\n   - The player who makes the party (the host) keeps the party's settings and its list of places.\n6. **Scaling:** monster life rises by 100% and damage by 50% for each extra hero in the same place. Bosses rise by 100%\n   and 25%. All four are knobs, calibrated with the autopilot (section 6).\n7. **Rewards are personal, as in single player.** Each hero rolls their own loot, which only they see. Experience from a\n   kill goes in full to every hero near it (section 8).\n8. **Phases 1 and 2 pay off on their own, even if Phase 0 changes the network design.**\n   - Phase 1: the world no longer depends on the screen. The doctrine already promises this, and Emberdeep doesn't keep\n     it yet.\n   - Phase 2: several heroes in one world. The owner can play with up to three autopilot companions before any\n     networking exists.\n\n## Read this first\n\n- Read `CLAUDE.md`, `DOCTRINE.md`, `src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`, then this file.\n- **One phase per pull request, in order (section 15).**\n  - Phase 0 is a Temporary lab and takes no version bump.\n  - Every other phase takes the next minor version when it merges.\n  - Section 19 logs where the work stands.\n- **References and line numbers:**\n  - Line numbers are as of v0.46.1. Search by name when they have moved.\n  - `NN:line` is `src/emberdeep/NN-*.js`. `F:line` is `engine/my-3d2dge.js`.\n- **Engine work goes into the full engine only.** The agent edition stays a strict subset (`npm run test:agent`) and\n  gets no co-op until a kit needs it.\n- **The co-op card.** The manual's budgets are nearly full:\n======MATRIX\n# Handoff: what the cc-matrix run's 96 builders patched, hit and asked for\n\n> **Status (2026-11-04, at v0.86.0):** open. Written from the cc-matrix run of Nov 3 and 4 (96 builds on the handoff\n> package v0.85.2). v0.86.0 landed the quick ones (below); everything else is here, in the order to take it. It is\n> temporary: when an item lands, its line goes (say so in the changelog); when none is left, this file goes. What we\n> decided not to do is in `docs/FEEDBACK-LOG.md` (F8).\n\n## Where this comes from\n\n- **The run:** `~/dev/cc-matrix/runs/<model>.build__<time>__<id>__<Game>/`, the folders from `20261103-174700` on: 24\n  plans by Opus 5.5 (max), each built by Opus xhigh, Opus medium, Sonnet medium and Haiku medium. Each build's\n  `work/exit-interview.json` has six `engineFeedback` answers (liked, stuck or guessed wrong, built yourself, hardest,\n  what you changed, one thing to change), and `work/<Game>/` holds its copy of the engine as the builder left it.\n  `~/dev/cc-matrix/audit.json` is the run's audit (ENGINE-1 to ENGINE-4, TEST-1: tagged from the answers, not\n  reproduced). Weigh Opus first, then Sonnet; Haiku cut features and says so.\n- **The patches, measured:** each build's `my-3d2dge.js` and `modules/*.js` diffed against v0.85.2's\n  (`git show 4390a98:dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js`), every hunk placed in its engine section. 79 of 96 builds changed the\n  engine (all 48 Opus, 20 Sonnet, 11 Haiku). Builds that changed each place (engine files only; more patched it in\n  their game's code):\n  | What | Builds |\n  |---|---|\n  | the button symbols' default (`STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs`, `Input.glyphs`) | 53 |\n  | the Humanoid (weapons 12, hats 13, outfits 6, draw hooks 7, held poses 4) | 37 |\n  | an audio bus or a voice bus | 35 |\n  | the font tables (✕ ○ □ △ in 29) | 32 |\n  | `E.Dialog`'s voice per letter | 28 |\n  | the AI Hub's Create + Options kept from the pause | 20 |\n  | the players' section (glyphs for a joining player, half keyboards) | 12 |\n  | E.Shell (settings rows on any screen 8, a TV size 6) | 15 |\n  | `E.HOOKS` exported (a per-step hook), analog triggers | 8, 8 |\n  | the floor bakes' cache size raised (6 to 10-16) | 6 |\n- **Checked:** every bug below was read in the code at v0.85.2 and, where it runs headless, reproduced in Node; the\n  3D ones in Chromium (WebGL 2 on SwiftShader). Each says what it is: **confirmed**, **partly**, **by design** (then the\n  fix is a doc), or what the builder actually hit.\n\n## Already done in v0.86.0 (don't redo)\n\nThe font's ✕ ○ □ △ − and marks and accents (both editions); a standard setting's default from the game\n(`menu.settings: [{ key: 'glyphs', value: 'playstation' }]`) and the controller's settings on every player;\n`E.Dialog`'s voice function, `say({ voice })`, `width`; `audio.bus()` and the voice bus; the rig's `NaN` from `vx`\nwithout `vy`; the music's look-ahead with an audio offset; a script's `tap`/`hold` no device, a tap leaving a hold,\n`feed('move')` a mouse move, a real mouse move setting `lastDevice`, a script's pad codes kept with no pad;\n`after`/`every` `{ keep: true }`; the floor bakes' least-recently-used drop; `window.Mocap`; the card's lines and D77;\nthe handoff README's 3D pointer and headless pitfalls. Tests: `matrixFixes` in `tools/contract-test.mjs`, part 5 of\n`tools/audio-test.mjs`.\n\n## Rules for this plan\n\n- **Reproduce first.** A failing check before the fix: in `tools/contract-test.mjs` (`matrixFixes`, or its Node part\n  for anything headless; its browser part for drawing), `tools/kit3d-test.mjs` for the 3D engine, `tools/views-test.mjs`\n  for mode7 and road. Run it against the old code once to see it fail.\n- **Headless stays the browser's twin** (principle 5). Never make a headless game behave differently from a page\n  (`game.go` keeps its fade; a test advances past it or sets `game.fadeTime = 0`).\n- **Make room before adding.** The kits sit near their budgets (cl100k, as `doctrine-check` counts; `gpt-tokenizer`'s\n  default `encode` is o200k and reads high). v0.86.1 gave each about 2,650 back (the fonts as digit rows): adventure\n  207,306 of 210,000, rpg 238,221 of 241,000, brawler 203,988 of 207,000, rts 204,769 of 208,000, shooter 220,660 of\n  224,000, sim 206,399 of 210,000; the API card 21,027 of 21,100; the agent header 9,974 of 10,000. The card's \"Common\n  mistakes\" (Part 8) and any line a part adds come out of that room, not new raises.\n- **Both editions:** a bug in code the agent edition has is fixed there too; an addition may stay the full engine's\n  (the agent edition's API is a subset: `npm run test:agent`).\n- **Process:** `CLAUDE.md`: a version per pull request, its changelog lines, `node tools/build.mjs`,\n  `npm run test:changed`, squash merge, then `npm test` in the background. Each budget raise or design call in its\n  own commit, with its line in `docs/DECISIONS.md`. A big design (marked **prototype**) starts as a Temporary lab\n  (`src/labs.json`: its date, the question it answers) for the owner to judge, and graduates only on their word.\n\n## Part 3: 2D rendering and cameras\n\n- **3.4 A perspective camera for 2D games** (**prototype**; the most asked-for addition: 13 runs, and all four Big Buck\n  Hunter and all four Time Crisis builders wrote their own renderer). First, point light-gun and first-person games at\n  the 3D engine (the README does now). Then a light-gun gallery on the mode7 module as a Temporary lab: `m7.pick(x, y)`\n  (the billboard or shape under the crosshair, by its drawn box), `m7.wall`/`m7.quad` (world polygons sorted with the\n  billboards), shapes with their params (3.5), lights; or a perspective `E.View`. The owner picks.\n\n## Part 4: the Humanoid\n\n- **4.1 Open registries** (37 builds changed the rig: weapons in 12, hats in 13). The weapon list is closed\n  (`['sword', 'gun', 'staff']` in the constructor). `E.Humanoid.weapon(id, { length, rest, grip, draw })`,\n  `E.Humanoid.hat(id, draw)`, a draw hook with the projected joints (`o.draw: { under, over }`) for outfits and\n  accessories, named held poses as data (`E.Humanoid.pose(id, (J, w) => ...)`, blended by weight), and a whole-body\n  tilt and roll for dives, falls and acrobatics (Europhoria, Olympiyikes). Every built-in weapon, hat and pose moves","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ea1b61d-e1b8-4350-be76-ae4a4cbc51a9","uuid":"0d4251f5-ea1e-4722-ac9c-cebfdcfe0682","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:42.022Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Handoff: co-op (up to four players, each free to go anywher