{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tq5eKkXoWkNJzUWZoZkZVp","type":"tool_result","content":"  tilt and roll for dives, falls and acrobatics (Europhoria, Olympiyikes). Every built-in weapon, hat and pose moves\n  onto the same registries (the proof that they are enough).\n- **4.2 `Math.random` in the rig** (confirmed: one call in the constructor, one in the blink): give each rig its own\n  stream (a seed in its options, else a counter), so a headless run's rig state is the same twice. TideCrisis reseeded\n  every rig's clock by hand.\n\n## Part 5: sound\n\n- **5.1 Effects with pan and length:** `sfx(name, { pan, loop })` returning `{ stop(), set({ pan, vol }) }`, and\n  continuous loops (an engine, rain) that follow the bus.\n- **5.2 Instruments of a game's own** (9 builds extended the synth): `audio.instrument(name, (ctx, bus, time, freq,\n  dur, vol) => ...)`, usable as a music track's `wave`.\n\n## Part 6: menus and the Shell for a TV\n\n- **6.1 `E.Menu` at scale 1 with no scrolling** (TV readability came up in 36 runs; four builds wrote their own menu).\n  `new E.Menu(game, items, { scale, rows })` with the list scrolling under its cursor, the mouse hovering, the rows\n  from `update` (2.1).\n- **6.2 The Shell's 14 px text:** a theme scale for ten feet (`new E.Shell(game, { theme: { scale } })`, or `tv: true`:\n  OutBloom's `clamp(18px, 1.6vw, 30px)`); `theme.font` is a family, not a CSS shorthand (two runs' menus came out tiny):\n  warn on a shorthand.\n- **6.3 Settings rows on any Shell screen** (8 builds): a screen of the game's own lists setting keys as rows\n  (`rows: ['glyphs', 'invertY']`), drawn and changed as the settings screen's are.\n- **6.4 The font's characters on the card**, and what it does with any other (`?`), so a game knows before it prints.\n\n## Part 7: the 3D engine\n\n- **7.5 A racer's track** (**prototype**; 7 of 8 WipeoutHD and TrackMania builders wrote their own track physics;\n  ribbon tracks, loops and branches asked 12 times). A ribbon track with loops and any banking, its own colliders, and\n  queries in track space (along, across, up); and a road (`E.Road`) that branches at a junction or runs several\n  tracks (OutBloom, DenshaDeHalo forked road.js for it). Each as a Temporary lab first.\n\n## Order and size\n\nLanded: Part 9 (the suites' own failures) in v0.85.3, Part 0 (the room) in v0.86.1, and in v0.87.0 1.1 to 1.4, 1.10,\nPart 2, 3.5, 3.6, 7.1 to 7.4 and Part 8; in v0.88.0 1.5 to 1.9, 3.1 to 3.3 and 3.7 to 3.11 (3.7's default: F8.9). Then\nParts 4 to 6 (medium); the prototypes 3.4 and 7.5 last, for the owner.\n======REVIEW\n# Handoff: the owner's review pass after the genre plan\n\n> **Status (2026-10-29, at v0.84.1):** parts 2 to 5, an agent's, have landed (in the order 5, 2, 4, 3, as the owner\n> said); parts 1 and 6, the owner's, are what is left.\n\nWhat the genre plan (`docs/HANDOFF-GENRES.md`, folded in at v0.81.2 and, after a last version with M16's row, at\nv0.81.5; its rules now in `docs/KITS.md`, its full text in `git show 24aee29:docs/HANDOFF-GENRES.md`) left for the\nowner. The plan built what each milestone needed and deferred the trimming to a review after the owner had played the\nkits (the owner's word, 2026-10-28). It is temporary: when a part lands, its line goes; when none is left, this file\ngoes.\n\n**In short.** Only the owner can do what is left, parts 1 and 6: play the kits, and read the calls made without them.\n(Part 5, a fix to `r.pick`, landed in v0.81.7; part 2, the budgets, in v0.82.0: D69 to D71; part 4, the CRAWLER and\nSPORTS kits, in v0.83.0 and v0.84.0; part 3, the benchmark's era briefs, run in v0.84.1: F7 in `docs/FEEDBACK-LOG.md`.)\n\n## 1. Play the kits (only the owner)\n\nThe Labs page's **To check** section holds 25 cards, each saying what to do and what to look for. Most want a phone or\na second player. A card goes once checked; what the owner wants changed becomes a plan or a fix.\n- **2D kits:** Roguelike on your phone; Puzzle (how the blocks handle, and versus); Arcade (the three classics);\n  Shooter (the horizontal stage); Brawler with a friend; Fighting (Street Fighter); Metroidvania (Super Metroid); Kart\n  (Super Mario Kart); Road (OutRun); RPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy); Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Fire Emblem);\n  RTS (Warcraft II, StarCraft); Sim (Theme Park); Point-and-click (Monkey Island); Pinball (Pinball Fantasies); Artillery\n  (Worms); Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa); Stealth's music (the chase's layers); Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA\n  Jam: with friends).\n- **3D kits:** Horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark); 3D Action's lock-on (Zelda); Racing (Mario Kart 64); Flight\n  (Star Fox 64); Crawler (Eye of the Beholder, Dungeon Master: real time and turns).\n- **The Vehicle Lab** (Labs, Temporary, `/vehicle-lab`): drive both karts (Tab switches) to settle D62. B is what\n  `world.vehicle` does (a rolling body, arcade handling); A is Rapier's ray-cast vehicle on springs. If B, the lab goes;\n  if A's weight is worth having, that becomes a plan.\n\n## 6. Calls made without the owner, to read (each reversible: a revert undoes it)\n\nLogged in `docs/DECISIONS.md` and flagged in their pull requests (v0.79.0 to v0.83.0):\n- **D60:** the 3D engine's manual allowed 8,000 tokens (it is at 6,807). **D61:** the 3D horror kit's budget, 256,000.\n- **D62:** the 3D engine's vehicles are a rolling body with arcade handling, not Rapier's ray-cast vehicle. Settle it in\n  the Vehicle Lab (part 1).\n- **D63, D64:** the 3D kits' budgets raised, because every 3D kit carries the whole 3D engine; D70 put the four that\n  never drive or fly back under their old budgets.\n- **D72 (v0.83.0):** the 3D CRAWLER kit's budget, 250,000.\n- **D69 to D71 (v0.82.0, the budgets):** the 2D kits' 200,000 back, with six (RPG, SHOOTER, ADVENTURE, SIM, RTS,\n  BRAWLER) keeping budgets of their own; the 3D kits' budgets back from D63 by a 3D diet; a kit's card keeping its own\n  genre's playbook rows and modules.\n- **D65, D68:** the genre plan folded into `docs/KITS.md` and this file, and back for one version to carry its M16 row.\n- **D66:** the era briefs' runs skipped, with no answer in fifteen minutes; run after all on the owner's yes (F7,\n  v0.84.1).\n- **D67 (v0.81.3, #191):** the music clock now meets the sound at once when it is more than 10 ms off for two frames\n  running (it smoothed anything under 100 ms, so a slip took 9 to 14 frames to meet). The audio suite's clock check now\n  allows the sound past 10 ms only in stretches of at most 6 frames, in under 1% of the frames, whatever began them. It\n  had excused only the frames after the sound device's slips, and failed in a slow container. This is an engine change\n  and a change to what a check measures, so it is the one to read first.\n===== F8\n238:### F7 · 2026-10-29 · the benchmark's five era briefs (Pac-Man, Street Fighter II, Super Mario Kart, Final Fantasy Tactics, Resident Evil)\n316:### F8 · 2026-11-04 · the cc-matrix run: 96 builds of 24 plans (Opus xhigh and medium, Sonnet medium, Haiku medium)\n### F8 · 2026-11-04 · the cc-matrix run: 96 builds of 24 plans (Opus xhigh and medium, Sonnet medium, Haiku medium)\n\n- **Builder:** Claude Code headless, run by the owner's cc-matrix dashboard (`~/dev/cc-matrix`): 24 plans by Opus 5.5\n  at max effort, each built four times, by Opus 5.5 at xhigh and medium, Sonnet 5.5 and Haiku 5.5 at medium, on\n  Nov 3 and 4. Each brief a spiritual successor to a game, a film or a show (The Wire, Time Crisis, Wipeout HD, Big\n  Buck Hunter, NBA Jam, Eye of the Beholder ...), controller first, for a TV, with an attract mode and the AI Hub's\n  multiplayer. Weighed Opus first, then Sonnet; Haiku cut features and said so.\n- **Handed:** the handoff package v0.85.2 from GitHub (its README, the cards, the engine readable, the kits).\n- **Built:** 96 games, every one starting headless without an error (the run's audit, `~/dev/cc-matrix/audit.json`);\n  the median Opus build about 1.7 MB. 79 changed the engine (all 48 Opus, 20 Sonnet, 11 Haiku), most the same few\n  lines over and over: measured by diffing each build's copy against v0.85.2 (`docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md`, Where this\n  comes from). What they liked most, nearly all of them: headless play (`game.advance`, `game.debug`).\n- **Acted on:** v0.86.0 (the quick ones: the glyphs default, the font's symbols, `Dialog`'s voice, `audio.bus`, the\n  rig's `NaN`, the music's offset, input's scripted taps, kept timers, the floor bakes, `window.Mocap`), and the plan\n  `docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md` (the rest: the hub's Create + Options, headless draws, `E.store`, the floor bake's zoom, a\n  perspective camera, the Humanoid's registries, the TV menus, the 3D engine's bugs, racing tracks).\n- **Not acted on (yet):**\n  - **F8.1** · a headless game that differs from the page · the run's audit asked that `game.go` switch scenes at once\n    in tests (21 runs waited out the fade without knowing). **No:** a headless game is the page's twin (principle 5:\n    the same inputs, the same state, with any renderer or none), so a replay or a test proves what a player sees. The\n    card and the handoff README now say to advance past the fade (`game.fadeTime`) or set it to 0.\n  - **F8.2** · maps beyond a height field · DragonQuiltBuilders (Opus xhigh and medium) built a voxel world and its own\n    renderer (\"the engine map is a height field\"); Brigantime (Opus medium) asked for hex TileMap floors with picking.\n    **Deferred:** a second source asks (the x-ray's exact boxes they also hit are in the plan, 3.8).\n  - **F8.3** · rigs beyond the Humanoid and the Blob · Brigantime (Opus xhigh): four-legged, winged and serpentine rigs\n    with named moves, \"a monster-heavy game has to build its whole bestiary\". **Deferred:** a second source asks.\n  - **F8.4** · the 3D engine's level as data that changes in play · EyeOfTheBoltHolder (Opus xhigh: secret walls,\n    trapdoors; Opus medium: a crawler's cells, doors and wall features), Skiller7 (Opus medium: a headless stand-in for\n    `World3D`'s level, rays and projection). **Deferred:** a second source asks, or the CRAWLER kit needs one.\n  - **F8.5** · cinematic cameras · Skiller7 (Opus xhigh): shots along a rail with line-of-sight checks. **Deferred.**\n  - **F8.6** · perception's tuning · Bentou2 (Opus xhigh): `E.Suspicion` never decays while the target is seen, so a\n    faint exposure still builds up. **Watch.**\n  - **F8.7** · the 50 ms frame cap · SnipeoutHD, Skiller7, EyeOfTheBoltHolder: under software rendering game time\n    crawls, so tests had to wait on state. **No:** by design (a frame past 50 ms gives up its time); the plan's card\n    line (Part 8) says to wait on state.\n  - **F8.8** · real devices · the goal checks asked for a real pad, sound heard and a person playing, which no headless\n    run can give. **No** for the engine (principle 10); the run's own fix is cc-matrix's.\n  - **F8.9** · the road square by default · OutBloom (Opus xhigh) and DenshaDeHalo (Opus xhigh) wrote square projections\n    for `E.Road` (it scales x by half the width and y by half the height: on a wide screen the world is squashed). The\n    plan (`docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md` 3.7) put it behind an option first, then the default with the ROAD kit retuned.\n    v0.89.0 has the option (`square: true`, and `road.ahead(h, r)` to draw the camera where the car stands at the screen's\n    foot on any screen). **Deferred**, the default: filmed on the ROAD kit, square needs the camera 1.8 times farther\n    back on a wide screen (the road narrow and far, the car off the screen) or a new field of view and camera height,\n    and the stretched road is the arcades' own look (OutRun). Which look the kit takes is the owner's call.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ea1b61d-e1b8-4350-be76-ae4a4cbc51a9","uuid":"ed3cf964-3b48-464d-9792-1d62e4cb2fde","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:46.349Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  tilt and roll for dives, falls and acrobatics (Europhoria, Olympiyikes). Every built-in weapon, hat and pose moves\n  onto the same registries (the proof that they are enough).\n- **4.2 `Math.random` in the rig** (confirmed: one call in the constructor, one in the blink): give each rig its own\n  stream (a seed in its options, else a counter), so a headless run's rig state is the same twice. TideCrisis reseeded\n  every rig's clock by hand.\n\n## Part 5: sound\n\n- **5.1 Effects with pan and length:** `sfx(name, { pan, loop })` returning `{ stop(), set({ pan, vol }) }`, and\n  continuous loops (an engine, rain) that follow the bus.\n- **5.2 Instruments of a game's own** (9 builds extended the synth): `audio.instrument(name, (ctx, bus, time, freq,\n  dur, vol) => ...)`, usable as a music track's `wave`.\n\n## Part 6: menus and the Shell for a TV\n\n- **6.1 `E.Menu` at scale 1 with no scrolling** (TV readability came up in 36 runs; four builds wrote their own menu).\n  `new E.Menu(game, items, { scale, rows })` with the list scrolling under its cursor, the mouse hovering, the rows\n  from `update` (2.1).\n- **6.2 The Shell's 14 px text:** a theme scale for ten feet (`new E.Shell(game, { theme: { scale } })`, or `tv: true`:\n  OutBloom's `clamp(18px, 1.6vw, 30px)`); `theme.font` is a family, not a CSS shorthand (two runs' menus came out tiny):\n  warn on a shorthand.\n- **6.3 Settings rows on any Shell screen** (8 builds): a screen of the game's own lists setting keys as rows\n  (`rows: ['glyphs', 'invertY']`), drawn and changed as the settings screen's are.\n- **6.4 The font's characters on the card**, and what it does with any other (`?`), so a game knows before it prints.\n\n## Part 7: the 3D engine\n\n- **7.5 A racer's track** (**prototype**; 7 of 8 WipeoutHD and TrackMania builders wrote their own track physics;\n  ribbon tracks, loops and branches asked 12 times). A ribbon track with loops and any banking, its own colliders, and\n  queries in track space (along, across, up); and a road (`E.Road`) that branches at a junction or runs several\n  tracks (OutBloom, DenshaDeHalo forked road.js for it). Each as a Temporary lab first.\n\n## Order and size\n\nLanded: Part 9 (the suites' own failures) in v0.85.3, Part 0 (the room) in v0.86.1, and in v0.87.0 1.1 to 1.4, 1.10,\nPart 2, 3.5, 3.6, 7.1 to 7.4 and Part 8; in v0.88.0 1.5 to 1.9, 3.1 to 3.3 and 3.7 to 3.11 (3.7's default: F8.9). Then\nParts 4 to 6 (medium); the prototypes 3.4 and 7.5 last, for the owner.\n======REVIEW\n# Handoff: the owner's review pass after the genre plan\n\n> **Status (2026-10-29, at v0.84.1):** parts 2 to 5, an agent's, have landed (in the order 5, 2, 4, 3, as the owner\n> said); parts 1 and 6, the owner's, are what is left.\n\nWhat the genre plan (`docs/HANDOFF-GENRES.md`, folded in at v0.81.2 and, after a last version with M16's row, at\nv0.81.5; its rules now in `docs/KITS.md`, its full text in `git show 24aee29:docs/HANDOFF-GENRES.md`) left for the\nowner. The plan built what each milestone needed and deferred the trimming to a review after the owner had played the\nkits (the owner's word, 2026-10-28). It is temporary: when a part lands, its line goes; when none is left, this file\ngoes.\n\n**In short.** Only the owner can do what is left, parts 1 and 6: play the kits, and read the calls made without them.\n(Part 5, a fix to `r.pick`, landed in v0.81.7; part 2, the budgets, in v0.82.0: D69 to D71; part 4, the CRAWLER and\nSPORTS kits, in v0.83.0 and v0.84.0; part 3, the benchmark's era briefs, run in v0.84.1: F7 in `docs/FEEDBACK-LOG.md`.)\n\n## 1. Play the kits (only the owner)\n\nThe Labs page's **To check** section holds 25 cards, each saying what to do and what to look for. Most want a phone or\na second player. A card goes once checked; what the owner wants changed becomes a plan or a fix.\n- **2D kits:** Roguelike on your phone; Puzzle (how the blocks handle, and versus); Arcade (the three classics);\n  Shooter (the horizontal stage); Brawler with a friend; Fighting (Street Fighter); Metroidvania (Super Metroid); Kart\n  (Super Mario Kart); Road (OutRun); RPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy); Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Fire Emblem);\n  RTS (Warcraft II, StarCraft); Sim (Theme Park); Point-and-click (Monkey Island); Pinball (Pinball Fantasies); Artillery\n  (Worms); Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa); Stealth's music (the chase's layers); Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA\n  Jam: with friends).\n- **3D kits:** Horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark); 3D Action's lock-on (Zelda); Racing (Mario Kart 64); Flight\n  (Star Fox 64); Crawler (Eye of the Beholder, Dungeon Master: real time and turns).\n- **The Vehicle Lab** (Labs, Temporary, `/vehicle-lab`): drive both karts (Tab switches) to settle D62. B is what\n  `world.vehicle` does (a rolling body, arcade handling); A is Rapier's ray-cast vehicle on springs. If B, the lab goes;\n  if A's weight is worth having, that becomes a plan.\n\n## 6. Calls made without the owner, to read (each reversible: a revert undoes it)\n\nLogged in `docs/DECISIONS.md` and flagged in their pull requests (v0.79.0 to v0.83.0):\n- **D60:** the 3D engine's manual allowed 8,000 tokens (it is at 6,807). **D61:** the 3D horror kit's budget, 256,000.\n- **D62:** the 3D engine's vehicles are a rolling body with arcade handling, not Rapier's ray-cast vehicle. Settle it in\n  the Vehicle Lab (part 1).\n- **D63, D64:** the 3D kits' budgets raised, because every 3D kit carries the whole 3D engine; D70 put the four that\n  never drive or fly back under their old budgets.\n- **D72 (v0.83.0):** the 3D CRAWLER kit's budget, 250,000.\n- **D69 to D71 (v0.82.0, the budgets):** the 2D kits' 200,000 back, with six (RPG, SHOOTER, ADVENTURE, SIM, RTS,\n  BRAWLER) keeping budgets of their own; the 3D kits' budgets back from D63 by a 3D diet; a kit's card keeping its own\n  genre's playbook rows and modules.\n- **D65, D68:** the genre plan folded into `docs/KITS.md` and this file, and back for one version to carry its M16 row.\n- **D66:** the era briefs' runs skipped, with no answer in fifteen minutes; run after all on the owner's yes (F7,\n  v0.84.1).\n- **D67 (v0.81.3, #191):** the music clock now meets the sound at once when it is more than 10 ms off for two frames\n  running (it smoothed anything under 100 ms, so a slip took 9 to 14 frames to meet). The audio suite's clock check now\n  allows the sound past 10 ms only in stretches of at most 6 frames, in under 1% of the frames, whatever began them. It\n  had excused only the frames after the sound device's slips, and failed in a slow container. This is an engine change\n  and a change to what a check measures, so it is the one to read first.\n===== F8\n238:### F7 · 2026-10-29 · the benchmark's five era briefs (Pac-Man, Street Fighter II, Super Mario Kart, Final Fantasy Tactics, Resident Evil)\n316:### F8 · 2026-11-04 · the cc-matrix run: 96 builds of 24 plans (Opus xhigh and medium, Sonnet medium, Haiku medium)\n### F8 · 2026-11-04 · the cc-matrix run: 96 builds of 24 plans (Opus xhigh and medium, Sonnet medium, Haiku medium)\n\n- **Builder:** Claude Code headless, run by the owner's cc-matrix dashboard (`~/dev/cc-matrix`): 24 plans by Opus 5.5\n  at max effort, each built four times, by Opus 5.5 at xhigh and medium, Sonnet 5.5 and Haiku 5.5 at medium, on\n  Nov 3 and 4. Each brief a spiritual successor to a game, a film or a show (The Wire, Time Crisis, Wipeout HD, Big\n  Buck Hunter, NBA Jam, Eye of the Beholder ...), controller first, for a TV, with an attract mode and the AI Hub's\n  multiplayer. Weighed Opus first, then Sonnet; Haiku cut features and said so.\n- **Handed:** the handoff package v0.85.2 from GitHub (its README, the cards, the engine readable, the kits).\n- **Built:** 96 games, every one starting headless without an error (the run's audit, `~/dev/cc-matrix/audit.json`);\n  the median Opus build about 1.7 MB. 79 changed the engine (all 48 Opus, 20 Sonnet, 11 Haiku), most the same few\n  lines over and over: measured by diffing each build's copy against v0.85.2 (`docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md`, Where this\n  comes from). What they liked most, nearly all of them: headless play (`game.advance`, `game.debug`).\n- **Acted on:** v0.86.0 (the quick ones: the glyphs default, the font's symbols, `Dialog`'s voice, `audio.bus`, the\n  rig's `NaN`, the music's offset, input's scripted taps, kept timers, the floor bakes, `window.Mocap`), and the plan\n  `docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md` (the rest: the hub's Create + Options, headless draws, `E.store`, the floor bake's zoom, a\n  perspective camera, the Humanoid's registries, the TV menus, the 3D engine's bugs, racing tracks).\n- **Not acted on (yet):**\n  - **F8.1** · a headless game that differs from the page · the run's audit asked that `game.go` switch scenes at once\n    in tests (21 runs waited out the fade without knowing). **No:** a headless game is the page's twin (principle 5:\n    the same inputs, the same state, with any renderer or none), so a replay or a test proves what a player sees. The\n    card and the handoff README now say to advance past the fade (`game.fadeTime`) or set it to 0.\n  - **F8.2** · maps beyond a height field · DragonQuiltBuilders (Opus xhigh and medium) built a voxel world and its own\n    renderer (\"the engine map is a height field\"); Brigantime (Opus medium) asked for hex TileMap floors with picking.\n    **Deferred:** a second source asks (the x-ray's exact boxes they also hit are in the plan, 3.8).\n  - **F8.3** · rigs beyond the Humanoid and the Blob · Brigantime (Opus xhigh): four-legged, winged and serpentine rigs\n    with named moves, \"a monster-heavy game has to build its whole bestiary\". **Deferred:** a second source asks.\n  - **F8.4** · the 3D engine's level as data that changes in play · EyeOfTheBoltHolder (Opus xhigh: secret walls,\n    trapdoors; Opus medium: a crawler's cells, doors and wall features), Skiller7 (Opus medium: a headless stand-in for\n    `World3D`'s level, rays and projection). **Deferred:** a second source asks, or the CRAWLER kit needs one.\n  - **F8.5** · cinematic cameras · Skiller7 (Opus xhigh): shots along a rail with line-of-sight checks. **Deferred.**\n  - **F8.6** · perception's tuning · Bentou2 (Opus xhigh): `E.Suspicion` never decays while the target is seen, so a\n    faint exposure still builds up. **Watch.**\n  - **F8.7** · the 50 ms frame cap · SnipeoutHD, Skiller7, EyeOfTheBoltHolder: under software rendering game time\n    crawls, so tests had to wait on state. **No:** by design (a frame past 50 ms gives up its time); the plan's card\n    line (Part 8) says to wait on state.\n  - **F8.8** · real devices · the goal checks asked for a real pad, sound heard and a person playing, which no headless\n    run can give. **No** for the engine (principle 10); the run's own fix is cc-matrix's.\n  - **F8.9** · the road square by default · OutBloom (Opus xhigh) and DenshaDeHalo (Opus xhigh) wrote square projections\n    for `E.Road` (it scales x by half the width and y by half the height: on a wide screen the world is squashed). The\n    plan (`docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md` 3.7) put it behind an option first, then the default with the ROAD kit retuned.\n    v0.89.0 has the option (`square: true`, and `road.ahead(h, r)` to draw the camera where the car stands at the screen's\n    foot on any screen). **Deferred**, the default: filmed on the ROAD kit, square needs the camera 1.8 times farther\n    back on a wide screen (the road narrow and far, the car off the screen) or a new field of view and camera height,\n    and the stretched road is the arcades' own look (OutRun). Which look the kit takes is the owner's call.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Tq5eKkXoWkNJzUWZoZkZVp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
