{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9jm3z9sqhSVcDPfFQy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KxmaktZfYMWTVC8q1xN6cy","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211923__xa49253/work/.research/hub/ai-hub && wc -l docs/session-hub.md docs/multiplayer-plan.md; sed -n 1,140p docs/session-hub.md | cut -c1-360","description":"Read how the hub runs session rounds"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22655,"cache_read_input_tokens":248568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":22655},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ea1b61d-e1b8-4350-be76-ae4a4cbc51a9","uuid":"77fd29c6-8f6e-4182-926c-170d2ca2fd8e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:29:50.588Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9jkpcDKQjG2EchoP9N","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KxmaktZfYMWTVC8q1xN6cy":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211923__xa49253/work/.research/hub/ai-hub && wc -l docs/session-hub.md docs/multiplayer-plan.md; sed -n 1,140p docs/session-hub.md | cut -c1-360","description":"Read how the hub runs session rounds"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KxmaktZfYMWTVC8q1xN6cy","type":"tool_result","content":"  246 docs/session-hub.md\n  605 docs/multiplayer-plan.md\n  851 total\n# AI Hub sessions: the hub's side\n\nWhat the hub does around a round, and how a round travels between the\ndevices. Games never need this page: the protocol a game follows is\n`lib/hub/README.md` (served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>), and that is the only\nthing the game factory is given. This page is for whoever changes the hub:\n`lib/hub/` (client, bridge, round, harness) and `assets/js/session/` (the\nsession page).\n\n## Around a round\n\nA game only plays its round. Everything around it belongs to the hub and is\nthe same for every game, so players learn it once.\n\n### Points and wins\n\n- Each round, a player scores **one point for every player they beat**. A\n  player who left or sat out without reporting counts as beaten; one who\n  reported and then left keeps their place. Tied players share a place. Every\n  round of four is worth three points to its winner, whatever the game.\n- **First place is a win**; in a team result (a shared world's `finish` with\n  `won`) only when the team won.\n- The **table** orders players by points, then wins, then the better average\n  place (`assets/js/session/score.js`).\n\n### Awards\n\n- **Game awards** come from a game's `result()` or `finish()`. The hub shows\n  them on the results, in the player's colour, and counts them for the\n  session.\n- **Session awards** are the hub's, given when a session ends:\n  **Showstopper** (most game awards), **Hot streak** (most wins in a row, at\n  least two) and **Steady hand** (best average place).\n\n### Ways to win a session\n\nThe host picks a format in the lobby:\n\n| Format | Ends | Winner |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Party** (default) | after 8 rounds | most points, after the three session awards add **3 bonus points** each, so the last word is never certain |\n| **Sprint** | after 5 rounds | most points |\n| **First to 12** | when someone has 12 points after a round | that player |\n| **Endless** | when the host ends it | most points |\n\nTies go to more wins, then the better average place, and otherwise stay ties.\n\n### The deal\n\nThe host's own picks come first: **Up next** in the lobby, the games the host\nchose (`state.queue`, at most twelve slugs), played in their order before\nRandom Mode deals. The host picks from every game that seats the players\npresent and that every seat's browser can run; the deal's filters and the\ntouch-screen rule narrow only Random Mode. A pick that does not seat the\nplayers yet keeps its place for a later round. A session hosted from a game's\npage in the Friends view (`/session?first=<slug>`) starts with that game in\nthe list, and the address drops it once the session has it. The round's\nstate says `picked: true`, and its card reads *The host's pick*. A pick is\nadded to the played list, so Random Mode does not deal it again in the same\ncycle.\n\nThen Random Mode deals every game that declares `hub:session`, seats the players\npresent and sits on the Games shelf (`assets/js/session/pick.js`). A game\nwhose `data/overrides.json` entry says `\"session\": \"0\"` is never dealt:\n`scripts/hub-check.mjs --record` writes that, with a `sessionNote`, for a game\nthat fails the check, and takes it off again when the game passes.\n\nParallel and ghost games, where each player has a world of their own, far\noutnumber the shared worlds, so the deal keeps them as two decks and\nalternates between them while both have games, starting with a shared world.\nEach deck is\nshuffled from the session's seed and played through before any of its games\ncomes round again.\n\nWithin a deck the deal counts titles, not builds: one build of each title\n(`lineageKey`: the same source, or for an original the same name), chosen at\nrandom, so a source with hundreds of runs is one game among the others.\n\nThe seats' browsers decide too. Each seat says in its presence whether it has\na WebGPU adapter (a real one, not a software fallback) and whether it has\nnothing but a touch screen. A game declaring `hub:webgpu` `required` is dealt\nonly when every seat has WebGPU; when a seat is touch-only, the deal keeps to\ngames tagged `touch` while any fit. The lobby's count of games uses the same\nrules, and counts titles, as the deal does.\n\nThe host can narrow the deal in the lobby, before the first round or back in\nthe lobby between rounds, with six rows of filters: the mode; the round's\nlength from `hub:session-minutes` (short is up to 3 minutes, medium 4 to 6,\nlong 7 or more, and a game that does not say counts as long, since it gets\nthe longest round limit); the catalogue's `genre`; its `model` (a game that\nnames two has both); its `effort`, least first (low, medium, high, xhigh,\nmax, ultra, then any other, A to Z); and the month it was `added` to the\nlibrary, newest first, a UTC month as the Explore timeline files arrivals.\nValues in one row are alternatives, the rows narrow together, and a row with\nnothing chosen keeps every game; a game the catalogue says nothing about in a\nrow (no model, say) is left out once that row has a choice. Each value shows\nhow many titles the deal would have with it alone chosen in its row; one that\nwould leave none can't be chosen. The other players see the filters as one\nline. They travel in the session state, so a seat that takes over as host\ndeals with them too.\n\n### Between rounds\n\nFast is the point. From one round's last whistle to the next round's\ncountdown takes about 25 seconds, less when everyone presses Ready:\n\n1. **Results**, the moment a round ends, over the game: places, points, stats\n   and awards, and the table moving (about 5 seconds).\n2. **The card** (the splash): a quick spin through the deck lands on the next\n   game and shows its name, its mode, `hub:session-goal` and\n   `hub:session-howto`, with a countdown that starts the round by itself: 10,\n   20 (the default) or 30 seconds, as the host sets it in the lobby, before\n   the first round or between rounds. The next game has been loading\n   underneath since the results appeared.\n   - **Ready** (Enter, or Cross on a controller): when every player has\n     pressed it, the round starts at once.\n   - **I need more time** (Triangle): adds 10 seconds, and tells the others\n     *A player needs more time* without saying who. While more than half the\n     players are asking, the countdown holds until fewer than half are.\n     Pressing it again, or Ready, takes it back.\n   - **The game's own card**: a game that connects with `card: true` shows\n     the card itself, in its own style (lib/hub/README.md, *The card*). The\n     hub's card stays up until that game calls `ready()`, then steps aside:\n     each seat's page sends its game `card` (the time left, who is ready,\n     whether the countdown holds) whenever it changes, and every second\n     while the countdown runs, and passes the game's `answer` on as that seat's press. The hub\n     reads no controller meanwhile, since the game does. A seat whose game\n     has not loaded yet, or does not show its own card, keeps the hub's.\n3. **Start**: the card lifts and the 3-2-1 begins as soon as every game has\n   loaded; a game still loading gets up to 20 seconds more, then its player\n   sits the round out.\n\nThe host can jump ahead (**Next game now**), skip a game, or end the session.\nAt the end, a final screen crowns the winner with the session awards and the\nround-by-round story.\n\n### The live scoreboard\n\nParallel and ghost games report `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ea1b61d-e1b8-4350-be76-ae4a4cbc51a9","uuid":"5e696afb-e7dd-4158-a61e-29b0444af0c2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:29:50.635Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  246 docs/session-hub.md\n  605 docs/multiplayer-plan.md\n  851 total\n# AI Hub sessions: the hub's side\n\nWhat the hub does around a round, and how a round travels between the\ndevices. Games never need this page: the protocol a game follows is\n`lib/hub/README.md` (served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>), and that is the only\nthing the game factory is given. This page is for whoever changes the hub:\n`lib/hub/` (client, bridge, round, harness) and `assets/js/session/` (the\nsession page).\n\n## Around a round\n\nA game only plays its round. Everything around it belongs to the hub and is\nthe same for every game, so players learn it once.\n\n### Points and wins\n\n- Each round, a player scores **one point for every player they beat**. A\n  player who left or sat out without reporting counts as beaten; one who\n  reported and then left keeps their place. Tied players share a place. Every\n  round of four is worth three points to its winner, whatever the game.\n- **First place is a win**; in a team result (a shared world's `finish` with\n  `won`) only when the team won.\n- The **table** orders players by points, then wins, then the better average\n  place (`assets/js/session/score.js`).\n\n### Awards\n\n- **Game awards** come from a game's `result()` or `finish()`. The hub shows\n  them on the results, in the player's colour, and counts them for the\n  session.\n- **Session awards** are the hub's, given when a session ends:\n  **Showstopper** (most game awards), **Hot streak** (most wins in a row, at\n  least two) and **Steady hand** (best average place).\n\n### Ways to win a session\n\nThe host picks a format in the lobby:\n\n| Format | Ends | Winner |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Party** (default) | after 8 rounds | most points, after the three session awards add **3 bonus points** each, so the last word is never certain |\n| **Sprint** | after 5 rounds | most points |\n| **First to 12** | when someone has 12 points after a round | that player |\n| **Endless** | when the host ends it | most points |\n\nTies go to more wins, then the better average place, and otherwise stay ties.\n\n### The deal\n\nThe host's own picks come first: **Up next** in the lobby, the games the host\nchose (`state.queue`, at most twelve slugs), played in their order before\nRandom Mode deals. The host picks from every game that seats the players\npresent and that every seat's browser can run; the deal's filters and the\ntouch-screen rule narrow only Random Mode. A pick that does not seat the\nplayers yet keeps its place for a later round. A session hosted from a game's\npage in the Friends view (`/session?first=<slug>`) starts with that game in\nthe list, and the address drops it once the session has it. The round's\nstate says `picked: true`, and its card reads *The host's pick*. A pick is\nadded to the played list, so Random Mode does not deal it again in the same\ncycle.\n\nThen Random Mode deals every game that declares `hub:session`, seats the players\npresent and sits on the Games shelf (`assets/js/session/pick.js`). A game\nwhose `data/overrides.json` entry says `\"session\": \"0\"` is never dealt:\n`scripts/hub-check.mjs --record` writes that, with a `sessionNote`, for a game\nthat fails the check, and takes it off again when the game passes.\n\nParallel and ghost games, where each player has a world of their own, far\noutnumber the shared worlds, so the deal keeps them as two decks and\nalternates between them while both have games, starting with a shared world.\nEach deck is\nshuffled from the session's seed and played through before any of its games\ncomes round again.\n\nWithin a deck the deal counts titles, not builds: one build of each title\n(`lineageKey`: the same source, or for an original the same name), chosen at\nrandom, so a source with hundreds of runs is one game among the others.\n\nThe seats' browsers decide too. Each seat says in its presence whether it has\na WebGPU adapter (a real one, not a software fallback) and whether it has\nnothing but a touch screen. A game declaring `hub:webgpu` `required` is dealt\nonly when every seat has WebGPU; when a seat is touch-only, the deal keeps to\ngames tagged `touch` while any fit. The lobby's count of games uses the same\nrules, and counts titles, as the deal does.\n\nThe host can narrow the deal in the lobby, before the first round or back in\nthe lobby between rounds, with six rows of filters: the mode; the round's\nlength from `hub:session-minutes` (short is up to 3 minutes, medium 4 to 6,\nlong 7 or more, and a game that does not say counts as long, since it gets\nthe longest round limit); the catalogue's `genre`; its `model` (a game that\nnames two has both); its `effort`, least first (low, medium, high, xhigh,\nmax, ultra, then any other, A to Z); and the month it was `added` to the\nlibrary, newest first, a UTC month as the Explore timeline files arrivals.\nValues in one row are alternatives, the rows narrow together, and a row with\nnothing chosen keeps every game; a game the catalogue says nothing about in a\nrow (no model, say) is left out once that row has a choice. Each value shows\nhow many titles the deal would have with it alone chosen in its row; one that\nwould leave none can't be chosen. The other players see the filters as one\nline. They travel in the session state, so a seat that takes over as host\ndeals with them too.\n\n### Between rounds\n\nFast is the point. From one round's last whistle to the next round's\ncountdown takes about 25 seconds, less when everyone presses Ready:\n\n1. **Results**, the moment a round ends, over the game: places, points, stats\n   and awards, and the table moving (about 5 seconds).\n2. **The card** (the splash): a quick spin through the deck lands on the next\n   game and shows its name, its mode, `hub:session-goal` and\n   `hub:session-howto`, with a countdown that starts the round by itself: 10,\n   20 (the default) or 30 seconds, as the host sets it in the lobby, before\n   the first round or between rounds. The next game has been loading\n   underneath since the results appeared.\n   - **Ready** (Enter, or Cross on a controller): when every player has\n     pressed it, the round starts at once.\n   - **I need more time** (Triangle): adds 10 seconds, and tells the others\n     *A player needs more time* without saying who. While more than half the\n     players are asking, the countdown holds until fewer than half are.\n     Pressing it again, or Ready, takes it back.\n   - **The game's own card**: a game that connects with `card: true` shows\n     the card itself, in its own style (lib/hub/README.md, *The card*). The\n     hub's card stays up until that game calls `ready()`, then steps aside:\n     each seat's page sends its game `card` (the time left, who is ready,\n     whether the countdown holds) whenever it changes, and every second\n     while the countdown runs, and passes the game's `answer` on as that seat's press. The hub\n     reads no controller meanwhile, since the game does. A seat whose game\n     has not loaded yet, or does not show its own card, keeps the hub's.\n3. **Start**: the card lifts and the 3-2-1 begins as soon as every game has\n   loaded; a game still loading gets up to 20 seconds more, then its player\n   sits the round out.\n\nThe host can jump ahead (**Next game now**), skip a game, or end the session.\nAt the end, a final screen crowns the winner with the session awards and the\nround-by-round story.\n\n### The live scoreboard\n\nParallel and ghost games report `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KxmaktZfYMWTVC8q1xN6cy","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
