{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DKqBhaChqYCjCxSiz2HvML","type":"tool_result","content":"277:- `input.anyPressed()`: any action pressed this step (title screens: \"press any button\")\n364:- `E.font.title(g, s, x, y, o = {})`: Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge. title(g, 'CASTLE OF NIGHT', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: ['#fff6c8', '#ffd36a', '#e07a2a'], outline: '#1a0c10', depth: 3, depthColor: '#6a2a1a', align: 'center' })\n493:- `game.menu(name, o)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, waits under the settings. menu('settings') or menu('controls') opens that screen. Without a menu (menu: false, or the agent edition) it pauses a scene in play. o: { pause: false } opens it over a world that keeps stepp\n1177:- `new E.Shell(game, o = {}) → shell`: new E.Shell(game, { theme, screens: { name: spec }, settings: { items, onChange(settings, key) }, store: 'shell-settings', meters: .125, standard: true }): an HTML interface over the game (see the section's header)\n1178:- `new E.Shell({ theme })`: CSS variables (ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont)\n1179:- `new E.Shell({ screens })`: specs ui.show(name) starts from (the pause screen follows game.paused; screens: { pause: false } leaves it out)\n1180:- `new E.Shell({ settings })`: settings.items: the game's own settings, { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', min, max, step, options, value } (kept in game.settings, beside the standard ones)\n1181:- `new E.Shell({ store, meters })`: store: where an older version saved them; meters: meters per world unit for distances\n1182:- `new E.Shell({ standard })`: false leaves out the standard menu (the pause screen's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit, and the settings' groups)\n1195:- `shell.show(name, spec = {})` †: open a screen: 'title' | 'pause' | 'settings' | 'help' | 'results' | 'map' or a name of your own, from spec over o.screens[name]. A spec: { kicker, title, text (strings or functions), actions: [[label, fn] or { label, fn, act, primary, disabled }], back (fn for cancel) }. From another screen it stacks (back() returns); shown again, it updates (the new spec over the old). 'setti\n1196:- `shell.show('title')` †: { name, subtitle, meta: [...], footer }\n1197:- `shell.show('help')` †: { keys: [[key, what]], sections: [[heading, text]] }\n1198:- `shell.show('results')` †: { emblem, stats: [[value, label]], medals: [[label, earned]] }\n1199:- `shell.show('map')` †: { map, at, markers (a list or a function), labels: [[text, x, y]], onPick(x, y) }\n\n53:- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild, Arkham) wants an HTML HUD instead: `E.Shell` (below),\n84:- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (when your `E.Shell` has one) and Quit to title (it asks first; Keep playing has the focus). Arrows,\n110:- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n311:### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n312:A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or focus handling. It shows the game's state and holds none of it: you pass values in each step and \n313:- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} }, screens: { pause: {...}, help: {...} } })`. It takes over from the engine's own and keeps the st\n660:- The camera follows the player and the level is visible. The HUD uses screen pixels (`r.text`, `r.overlay`), anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`, or for a modern game `E.Shell`'s HTML pieces (`ui.hud` every step). With `expand: true`, look at it on a wide monitor and on a phone both ways (`tools/check.\n\n1002:      b.style.cssText = 'width:' + size + 'px;height:' + size + 'px;border-radius:50%;border:2px solid rgba(255,255,255,.45);background:rgba(20,16,40,.55);color:#fff;font:600 ' + Math.round(size / 5) + 'px system-ui,sans-serif;touch-action:none;user-selec\n1011:      b.style.cssText = 'min-width:44px;height:44px;padding:0 10px;border-radius:10px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.4);background:rgba(20,16,40,.55);color:#fff;font:600 11px system-ui,sans-serif;touch-action:none';\n1365: *   { font: 'normal' | 'tiny', outline: '#0b0814' | false, shadow: '#000' (1 px drop shadow), gradient: ['#fff', '#8ad'] (top to bottom),\n1827:        if (o.label) E.font.text(g, o.label, cx, cy - 13, color, { align: 'center', font: 'tiny', shadow: '#000', outline: false });\n1833:        if (o.label) E.font.text(g, o.label, clamp(ax - c * 10, 14, W - 14), clamp(ay - s * 10 - 3, 2, H - 8), color, { align: 'center', font: 'tiny', shadow: '#000', outline: false });\n2283:    _errBox.style.cssText = 'position:fixed;left:8px;right:8px;top:8px;z-index:99999;max-height:45vh;overflow:auto;background:rgba(40,0,8,.95);color:#ffd6d6;border:2px solid #ff5a5a;border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;font:12px/1.45 ui-monospace,Menlo,Con\n2999:    if (this.shownNote && this.real < this.shownNote.end) { const n = this.shownNote; this.r.overlay(g => E.font.text(g, n.text, this.r.W / 2, 3, '#ffffff', { align: 'center', shadow: '#000', outline: false, font: 'tiny' })); }\n3001:      this.r.overlay(g => { const w = E.font.width(t, { font: 'tiny' }) + 6;