{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WStNN5ynUsGkpUxQxMDUza","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PLAN: an original open-world mercenary game inspired by Far Cry 2\n2\t\n3\tYou build this alone, from this plan and TASK.md. Nobody can answer questions, so where the plan is silent, decide and move on.\n4\t\n5\t- **Read before coding:** sections 1 and 2, and Appendix A. Read the rest when your milestone (section 15) reaches it.\n6\t- **Paths** are relative to your dev folder (the subfolder TASK.md tells you to create). \"The deliverable\" is the single HTML file TASK.md names, one level above the dev folder.\n7\t- **The title is yours.** Follow TASK.md's title rule (brainstorm, then pick with a real random number generator). Nothing in this plan depends on the title. This plan just says \"the game\".\n8\t- **VERIFIED** means the director ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04, on engine v0.88.0 (commit `0a46310`), on WebGL 2, and on WebGPU where stated. Trust those and spend your effort elsewhere.\n9\t- **Everything is required unless marked (Stretch).** Section 15 says what to cut if you must. Whatever you cut, cut it whole: TASK.md allows no stubs, and no line of dialogue may point at something that isn't there.\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## 1. The game\n14\t\n15\t**Pitch.** You are a contractor with a ruined lung, sent into a burned-out republic on the shore of a dead inland sea to kill the old engineer who drained it. Two armies pay you to betray each other. Everything you touch wears out: your rifle, your truck, your lungs, your friends. Fire is your best weapon and your worst neighbour. At the end you are standing at a dam with your hands on a wheel, and you have to decide who gets to drown.\n16\t\n17\t**Genre:** open-world first-person shooter with survival pressure (guns that fail, a sickness, friends who can die for good).\n18\t\n19\t**Perspective:** first person, always. Your eyes never leave your head: no cutscene cameras, no third-person toggle, no fade-to-talk. Conversations happen while you stand there and can look around. Why: the source's identity lives in the hands and the eyes, and the engine ships a 2 km first-person open-world kit that already moves, shoots and streams well.\n20\t\n21\t**The five pillars. Every feature must serve one.**\n22\t1. **Fire is the weather of war.** Dry grass, wind and one spark redraw the map. Burn scars stay.\n23\t2. **Everything wears out.** Guns jam and split, trucks smoke and stall, lungs fail, friends die. You repair, replace and ration.\n24\t3. **Your hands are the interface.** Map, locator, monocular, syrette and pliers are objects you hold, and the world keeps moving while you hold them. No minimap, no floating markers, almost no HUD.\n25\t4. **Friends are your save system and your guilt.** Buddies drag you out of fights. You have to drag them out too, or end it for them. They stay dead. At the end they sell you.\n26\t5. **No clean hands.** Both sides are the same, and every contract makes things worse. People talk about it as if it were paperwork.\n27\t\n28\t**Taken from the source (mechanics and mood only):** spreading grass fire used as a weapon; weapon decay and jamming; a recurring sickness managed with rationed medicine; a real-time map in the hand; first-person field surgery; rescuing buddies with permanent death; choosing one of several mercenaries; two interchangeable factions; uncut diamonds as money with a beeping locator; safe houses with a bed and a time-skip; guard posts on the roads; a charismatic villain you came to kill; bleak, literary, weary writing.\n29\t\n30\t**Must be ours (TASK.md: an original game, not a recreation).** All names, characters, story, map, levels, art, music and sounds in this plan are original. Do not use the source's proper nouns or lookalikes: its country and towns, its factions (APR, UFLL), the Jackal, its characters (Reuben, Josip, Nasreen, Marty, Frank, Hakim, Paul and the rest), its opening scene, its tapes, its ending, its logo or its music. Do not set the game in a real country or name a real army. If in doubt, invent.\n31\t\n32\t**Where we beat the source.** It is famous for three annoyances. Do not repeat them:\n33\t- **Guard posts that refill the moment you drive past.** Ours stay cleared until you sleep, and they may not refill even then.\n34\t- **Everyone shooting you on sight.** Ours check your papers (section 8).\n35\t- **Malaria on a dumb timer.** Our sickness responds to what you do: smoke and dust bring it on, and you can feel it coming (section 4).\n36\t\n37\t**Length:** 60 to 90 minutes for a first run through the whole story, with every feature used at least once. A second player can finish faster.\n38\t\n39\t**Three moments the systems must be able to produce without a script.** If these can happen, the game is working:\n40\t- You're crouched in reeds at night with a patrol 30 m away and your lung at 90. You have one ampoule. You hear your breath catch.\n41\t- You put two bursts into a man, your rifle clicks dead, and while you rack it the grass behind him goes up, and the wind is coming your way.\n42\t- You're on your back in the dirt, and through the smoke you see your friend running toward you. You owe them for the last time, too.\n43\t\n44\t---\n45\t\n46\t## 2. Foundation\n47\t\n48\t### 2.1 Get the pieces\n49\t\n50\t```sh\n51\t# in the dev folder\n52\tgit clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git engine-repo\n53\tgit -C engine-repo fetch --depth 1 origin 0a463102661e66de714aa76b321219f20028ab04\n54\tgit -C engine-repo checkout -q FETCH_HEAD        # v0.88.0: what this plan was verified on\n55\tmkdir -p engine hub src test\n56\tcp engine-repo/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine-repo/engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js engine/\n57\tcp -r engine-repo/vendor/three-0.182.0 engine-repo/vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3 engine/\n58\tcp engine-repo/src/starter3d/00-shell.js engine-repo/src/starter3d/30-shooter.js engine-repo/src/starter3d/90-start.js src/\n59\tfor f in session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js; do curl -fsSO --output-dir hub https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n60\t```\n61\t\n62\tThe engine files in `engine/` are yours to patch. Keep a one-line log of every patch in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`: TASK.md asks for it in Engine Feedback. Delete `engine-repo/` when you finish.\n63\t\n64\t**The stack.** The handoff package's 2D engine still runs the game loop, scenes, input, menus, settings, sound and the HUD. `E.World3D` (three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3) draws and simulates the 3D world under it. This is the route the handoff README's step 7 names for first person. Do not build on the 2D views, and do not write your own renderer.\n65\t\n66\t**What to read in `engine-repo/`, and nothing more:**\n67\t- `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` lines 1-264 (the whole 3D manual).\n68\t- `dist/handoff/API.md`: the Game, Input, Dialog, Menu, Sound and Standard features sections.\n69\t- `dist/handoff/docs/ASSETS.md`: when you write props.\n70\t- Grep `dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md` for names; never read it whole.\n71\t- `docs/ENGINE-3D.md`, section \"Things to know\".\n72\t- `node dist/handoff/tools/asset-find.mjs \"tent\"` searches the CC0 prop store (trees, tents, crates, furniture). There are no modern vehicles or weapons in it: you draw those.\n73\t\n74\t### 2.2 Build into one file (VERIFIED)\n75\t\n76\tAn esbuild bundle of the kit booted from `file://` in about 7 s with no errors and no server. The file came to about 4.8 MB before any game code, with three.js and Rapier inlined, so it needs no CDN and no network. The hub's importer leaves embedded engines alone. Appendix A1 has the build script and page template.\n77\t\n78\t- Keep game code as numbered files in `src/`, concatenated in order into one ES module. Remove the kit's `const SHOOT = (() => {` wrapper, so everything lives at module top level.\n79\t- Suggested files: `00-shell`, `05-data` (tables only), `06-story` (text only), `10-land`, `20-soldier`, `30-player`, `40-weapons`, `45-fire`, `50-vehicles`, `55-ai`, `60-buddies`, `65-contracts`, `70-hud`, `75-screens`, `80-audio`, `85-net`, `88-bot`, `90-start`. `src/metas.html` holds the hub tags (section 13); `src/page.css` holds the CSS in A7.\n80\t- **Data lives in tables.** TASK.md wants the rest of the game buildable later with normal designer tools. So sites, roads, weapons, looks, vehicles, contracts, dialogue, barks, radio and the shop are plain data in `05-data` and `06-story`. Systems read those tables. Adding a contract or a site must not touch a system.\n81\t\n82\t### 2.3 The kit, section by section\n83\t\n84\t`src/30-shooter.js` is 2,088 lines and 200 KB. Read a section when you change it, not all of it at once. Line numbers are for v0.88.0.\n85\t\n86\t| Kit section | Lines | What to do |\n87\t|---|---|---|\n88\t| TUNING | 20-24 | Keep walk/run/eye constants. Delete shields, slide, zipline, wall climb. |\n89\t| THE BADLANDS: `baseH`, `height`, `color`, `OUTPOSTS`, `ROADS`, `route`, `planLand` | 28-146 | Keep `route`, the rasters and `planLand`. Rewrite `baseH`, `color` and the data (section 3). |\n90\t| Structure pieces: `quad`, `boxAx`, `cylAx`, `quadPts`, `B`, `beam`, `CYL`, `wall`, `shack`, `container`, `ladder`, `tower`, `tank` | 147-308 | Keep. Add `hull`, `lighthouse`, `dam`, `quay`, `tentRow`. Delete `zipline`. |\n91\t| `layout()` | 310-400 | Rewrite, one function per site. |\n92\t| `structureMesh` | 416-436 | Keep (one merged mesh, one material; kinds pick patterns). Add a rust kind if you want one. |\n93\t| SCATTER: `chunkItems`, `streamScatter` | 437-488 | New tree kinds, charred variants (section 6). |\n94\t| GRASS: `grassMeshes`, `buildGrass`, `drawGrass` | 489-547 | Add the burn mask (A4) and tall reeds. |\n95\t| FX: tracers, decals, `impactStar`, `drawFlashes` | 548-647 | Keep. |\n96\t| THE SOLDIER: `LOOKS`, `body`, `pose`, `deadPose`, `drawSoldier`, `drawGun` | 648-1015 | Add looks, heads, faces, new guns, crawl/burn/drag poses (section 8). Remove the sci-fi visors and masks. |\n97\t| WEAPONS: `WEAPONS`, `reloadCurve`, `fire`, `impact`, `explode` | 1016-1152 | New table, wear and jam (section 5). Remove shields, damage numbers and the loud hit-marker kinds. |\n98\t| THE PLAYER: `newState`, `look`, `move`, `actions`, `camStep` | 1153-1465 | Remove slide, climb, zip and the third-person view. Keep vault, mantle, ladders. Add tools, health, lung, vehicle seat. |\n99\t| RAIDERS: `spawnRaider`, `streamRaiders`, `raiderStep`, `raiderShoot`, `hurtPlayer` | 1466-1598 | Base for all soldier AI (section 8). |\n100\t| WORKING PARTS: doors, bins, loot | 1599-1695 | Keep doors. Bins become crates. |\n101\t| FIRST PERSON: `drawViewModel`, `keysAt`, `VM` | 1696-1792 | Add every new gun and every hand animation (section 4). |\n102\t| HUD | 1793-1885 | Rewrite to almost nothing (section 11). Delete the compass bar, outpost counter and control list. |\n103\t| SOUNDS | 1886-1919 | Base table; extend (section 12). |\n104\t| THE STEP: `step(dt, I)`, `keepPrev`, `onDraw`, `reset`, `run` | 1920-2052 | **Keep this seam.** `step(dt, I)` takes an input object `I = { move, mouse, stick2, pressed(a, w), down(a), use(a) }`. Real input, the bot, attract mode and tests all drive the game through `I`. `run(n)` is the replay proof: keep one and test it. |\n105\t| `touchLook`, `TOUCH`, `touchButtons` | 1928, 2053+ | Delete. TASK.md wants no touch controls. |\n106\t\n107\tFrom `00-shell.js` keep the `game` creation and helpers; delete the slice menu, `FamilyMenu` and title stage. `90-start.js` becomes your boot (section 13 and Appendix A6). Use **one world for everything**: the title screen and attract mode are that same world with a fixed camera. This saves boot time, which matters in section 13.\n108\t\n109\t### 2.4 Engine facts that will otherwise cost you hours\n110\t\n111\t1. **Units:** 16 units = 1 m. x east, y south, z up. Your own three.js meshes are in metres with y up: `E.World3D.toThree(x, y, z)`, or x/16, z/16, y/16.\n112\t2. **Fixed steps:** `update` runs at 1/120 s. A frame past 50 ms gives up its time, so under software rendering (about 4.5 fps here) game time crawls. Tests must wait on state, never on the clock.\n113\t3. **Make every mesh and material during boot, before `world.prepare()`.** A material first seen mid-game stalls. `prepare()` warms visible world meshes and all overlay meshes, even hidden ones. A world-scene pool mesh must be visible with a draw range of at least one triangle at that moment (the kit's trick: `setDrawRange(0, 3)`).\n114\t4. **Renderer rules:** node materials and TSL only. No `ShaderMaterial`, `onBeforeCompile`, `EffectComposer`, addons, compute or GPU read-back. An `InstancedMesh` over 1,000 instances must not use `DynamicDrawUsage`. For a dynamic pool, use a merged `BufferGeometry` with `setDrawRange`, as the kit's tracers and the flames in A4 do. A texture read in a vertex stage needs `.level(0)`.\n115\t5. **The pen** (`world.pen`, `overlay.pen`) draws for one frame. Call it each frame from `onDraw`. Draw changes nothing in the sim.\n116\t6. **Randomness:** the sim uses `E.rng(seed)` only, so `world.hash()` replays. `Math.random` is for pictures. In a shared-world session the host's sim uses its own `AIHub.rng(label)` stream.\n117\t7. **`game.go` fades for .22 s.** Set `game.fadeTime = 0` in tests.\n118\t8. **World-private fields used by recipes in Appendix A:** `world._sun`, `_sunDir`, `_ambient`, `_skyDome`, `_fog`, `terrain.mat`. They exist at the pinned commit, which is why you pin.\n119\t9. **`vehicleParts` is engine code** with three fixed kinds. Patch one line so your own models draw: `if (a.o.draw) a.o.draw(this.pen, a, t); else vehicleParts(this.pen, a, t);` at `my-3d2dge-3d.js` line 3006.\n120\t10. **`E.Menu` does not scroll** at v0.88.0. Keep lists to 6 rows or page them.\n121\t\n122\t---\n123\t\n124\t## 3. The world\n125\t\n126\t**The Republic of Lacustria**, a country named for a lake that isn't there. It is a high basin ringed by red rock. The **Amaranth**, an inland sea, was drained over forty years by a river diversion for cotton. Rusting trawlers sit on the dry bed, forty kilometres from any water. A lighthouse stands in a field. Toxic salt dust blows off the seabed. A ceasefire was signed in the spring, and nobody has stopped shooting.\n127\t\n128\tKeep the kit's 2,048 m square map (`SIZE = 32768`). Crossing it takes about 80 s by jeep, which is right for a slice with this many stops.\n129\t\n130\t### Sites (metres east, metres south)\n131\t\n132\tPut these in `SITES`. `planLand` nudges each to flat ground and flattens a pad of radius `r`.\n133\t\n134\t| id | Name | at | r | Held by | What stands there |\n135\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n136\t| `strip` | Vasco Strip | 1500, 1640 | 50 | nobody | Gravel runway, wrecked crop-duster, windsock. The prologue starts near here. |\n137\t| `mission` | Saint Odile's | 420, 1520 | 46 | clinic | Roofless chapel, 12 tents, water tank, the clinic jeep. Sister Perpetua. |\n138\t| `salt` | the Salt Shed | 1000, 1130 | 30 | safe house | Long tin shed, white salt heaps, on the Causeway. Refugee camp (30 tents) 120 m south. |\n139\t| `fleet` | the Fleet | 1180, 1290 | 90 | smugglers | Nine stranded trawler hulks, tilted, some you can walk inside. |\n140\t| `remnant` | the Remnant | 1120, 1400 | 130 | nobody | The last brine, knee deep. Flat water. The airboat lives here. |\n141\t| `reedcamp` | Reed Camp | 760, 1400 | 40 | Front | Boardwalks, tents, fuel barrels, in the tallest reeds. |\n142\t| `port` | Port Clement | 1640, 1010 | 80 | neutral, then ash | Quay wall facing nothing, 14 buildings, the Customs House (3 floors), the Harbourmaster bar, Mr. Sunday's boat in dry dock. |\n143\t| `k9` | Kilometre Nine | 1530, 600 | 50 | Authority HQ | Sandbags, containers, watch tower, flag, Captain Dray's office. |\n144\t| `red` | Red Steps | 430, 850 | 50 | Front HQ | Quarry terraces cut into a red mesa, one tunnel, watch tower, a schoolroom with a chalkboard. |\n145\t| `st4` | Station Four | 900, 720 | 44 | Front | Pump house, pipes, tank, a dry canal cut. |\n146\t| `lamp` | the Lamp | 1060, 660 | 26 | safe house | A 40 m lighthouse on a stone spur above the old north shore. Its light turns at night. You can navigate by it from anywhere. |\n147\t| `tannery` | the Tannery | 440, 1180 | 28 | safe house | Vats, hide racks, a shack, on the old west shore. |\n148\t| `watertower` | the Water Tower | 1380, 1600 | 22 | safe house | Tank on legs, shack under it. |\n149\t| `sill` | the Sill | 1000, 190 | 60 | Authority + Meridian | A dam wall across a canyon notch in the rim. Control house, helipad, dark reservoir behind. |\n150\t\n151\t**Roads** (`ROADS`, routed by the kit's A*):\n152\t- port-k9, k9-lamp, lamp-st4, st4-red, red-tannery, tannery-mission\n153\t- mission-watertower, watertower-strip, strip-port\n154\t- **the Causeway:** tannery-salt, salt-fleet (spur), salt-port, a raised road 2 m above the seabed running east-west\n155\t- lamp-sill, up the canyon\n156\t\n157\t**Guard posts** (`POSTS`: `{ on: [a, b], at: .5, faction }`):\n158\t- G1 port-k9 Authority\n159\t- G2 k9-lamp Authority\n160\t- G3 tannery-mission Front\n161\t- G4 st4-red Front\n162\t- G5 salt-port Authority\n163\t- G6 watertower-strip Front\n164\t\n165\t### Terrain (rewrite `baseH` and `color`)\n166\t\n167\t- **Bowl:** let `d = hypot((m-1050)/600, (n-1180)/430) + (fbm(m/300, n/300, 3) - .5) * .35` and `sea = 1 - sstep(.92, 1.08, d)`. Multiply the kit's relief by `(1 - sea)` and add `-14 * sea` metres, so the seabed is low and nearly flat.\n168\t- **Mesas:** keep the kit's two-tier mesas, but only where `sea == 0`, weighted toward the west and north-west (Red Steps).\n169\t- **Rim:** keep the kit's cliff ring. Cut a notch 280 m wide at `m ≈ 1000` on the north edge for the Sill's canyon.\n170\t- **Remnant:** make the terrain perfectly flat at one height inside its circle. There is no swimming. Draw a water plane a hand above it (A5). Walkers wade at 60%. The airboat is happy.\n171\t\n172\t`biomeAt(m, n)` returns one of: `salt` (seabed core, cracked, near white), `mud` (seabed rim, sparse sedge), `reeds` (a broad band inside the old shore in the south-west, from about (700, 1250) to (1000, 1560)), `grass` (default upland), `scrub`, `rock` (steep), `road`, `pad`, `brine`. The fire, vehicle surfaces, grass mesh and held map all read it.\n173\t\n174\t**Palette:** bleached gold grass `#c6aa64`, olive scrub `#8e8a55`, salt `#d9d2bd`, cracked mud `#a9967a`, reed `#b9b277`, rust `#7a3a1e`, oxide hull `#4f5c50`, ash `#191512`.\n175\t\n176\t**Scatter** (`chunkItems`):\n177\t- Flat-topped thorn trees: the `tree` shape with a low wide crown.\n178\t- `bottle` trees: your own `E.SHAPES` entry, a fat `lathe` trunk with a tiny crown. These are the silhouette the player remembers.\n179\t- Dead snags and rocks.\n180\t- Nothing on salt except wreckage: nets, anchors, ribs of small boats.\n181\t\n182\t**Structures** are merged into the kit's one mesh over `world.block` colliders, using its pieces plus yours:\n183\t- `hull(P, ...)`: 28 m long, 7 m beam, tilted 6-14°, plates as quads, rust patches, a hole in the side, an open hold.\n184\t- `lighthouse`, `quay`, `tentRow`.\n185\t- `dam`: wall 240 m wide and 46 m tall, three gates, a service stair, a top walkway, a control house with a big iron wheel, a separate reservoir water plane behind.\n186\t\n187\t**Art direction.** The engine's native look: low-poly pen-drawn bodies with outlines, pixel font, chunky textures. Keep the 3D picture at `balanced`: the kit already passes `res: 'balanced'`, and A7 makes the Settings row agree. It looks good at 1080p and still reads as this engine. Never smooth it.\n188\t\n189\t### Time, wind, weather\n190\t\n191\t- **Time of day:** 1 in-game hour = 90 s (slider). Use A3, whose keyframes were tuned on screenshots. Night must be dark but playable: moonlit blue, with fire and the Lamp as the bright things. Update the sun's direction every 5 game minutes, not every frame, or shadows shimmer.\n192\t- **Wind:** `windDir` drifts slowly and `wind` runs 0.2 to 1. Every 2 in-game hours a new target eases in over a minute. Show it only in the world: grass lean (drive the kit's sway from it), smoke drift (A4), windsocks at posts and strips, and a tiny pencil arrow on the map's compass rose.\n193\t- **Dust storm:** about one per game day, 2-3 minutes. Fog pulls in to about 120 m and turns orange (`world._fog.near/far`, `scene.fog.color`), sand particles, wind 1, lung ×4. Enemy sight halves for everybody. Fire runs fast. Tell the player in advance by radio.\n194\t\n195\t---\n196\t\n197\t## 4. The player and the hands\n198\t\n199\t### Controls (prompts always come from `game.input.label(action)`)\n200\t\n201\t| Action | DualSense | Keyboard and mouse |\n202\t|---|---|---|\n203\t| Move / look | L stick / R stick | WASD / mouse (captured on click) |\n204\t| Fire / aim | R2 / L2 | Left click / right click |\n205\t| Jump, vault, mantle | ✕ | Space |\n206\t| Crouch | ○ | C or Ctrl |\n207\t| Sprint | L3 | Shift |\n208\t| Use (tap); reload or clear jam when nothing to use | □ | E use; R reload or clear jam |\n209\t| Next weapon | △ | Q; 1, 2, 3 direct |\n210\t| Melee (machete) | R3 | V |\n211\t| Bottle / frag | L1 / R1 | F / G |\n212\t| Map / locator / monocular / syrette | D-pad up / right / left / down | M / N / B / H |\n213\t| Pause | Options | Esc |\n214\t| **In a vehicle:** gas / brake, reverse | R2 / L2 (analog: `input.value`) | W / S |\n215\t| Steer / look | L stick / R stick | A, D / mouse |\n216\t| Handbrake / exit | ✕ / □ | Space / E |\n217\t| Gun seat (technical) / radio | △ / L1 | Tab / L |\n218\t\n219\tUse one `KEYS` map for every scene. Bind only `Mouse0` plus the pad code for fire and aim. VERIFIED: `label` then gives `Left click`, or `R2` on a pad. The kit's extra `KeyJ` is what made it print \"J\". With a pad and glyphs `playstation`, VERIFIED labels are `R2 L2 ✕ □ △ ○`. With `xbox` they are `RT` and `A X`. Name every action in `menu.controls` so the Controls screen reads well.\n220\t\n221\t### Moving\n222\t\n223\tKeep the kit's walk 68, run 118, crouch 40, ADS 46 units/s, its vault, mantle and ladders. Sprint runs on stamina: 8 s to empty, 12 s to refill. Lower the jump a little. Kick doors with melee. Grounded, heavy, tired.\n224\t\n225\t### Holding things (pillar 3)\n226\t\n227\tEach tool is a stance on the overlay pen, keyframed with the kit's `keysAt`. Raising one takes .3-.45 s with a sound. The world never pauses. **Fire drops any tool at once.**\n228\t\n229\t- **Map (A2):** both hands, the gun gone. Walk at 60%. It shows contour lines, roads, site names, pencil circles on the current objective, your tagged soldiers as pencil ticks, a small arrow for you, a compass rose with the wind arrow. Your left wrist is in shot with a **watch**: the only clock in the game. Look input moves a pencil over the map instead of the camera. ✕ or left click pins a waypoint. L2/R2 or the wheel zoom between the whole basin and 3×. The crosshair and prompts hide. You can raise it while driving, and you will crash. That's the point.\n230\t- **Locator:** a chunky handset in the left hand, gun still in the right (hip fire only, spread ×2). A ring of 8 LEDs points to the pinned waypoint or current objective, with a small readout in metres (A2's method on a 128×64 canvas). Within 150 m of an unfound diamond case it beeps, from every 2 s down to every .15 s at 5 m, and a separate red LED blinks with it. It also rings for radio calls.\n231\t- **Monocular:** FOV 70 to 14, dark tube mask, slight sway. Held .6 s on a soldier, vehicle, ammo crate or fuel dump, it tags the thing: a pencil tick on the map and a small corner bracket seen only through the monocular. Tags clear when the site is clear.\n232\t- **Syrette (tap):** .9 s jab in the thigh, +40 HP.\n233\t- **Field surgery:** when health is in the last segment, the syrette button instead starts a 4-6 s operation you can't cancel. It restores you to 60 HP and costs no syrette. Pick one of four at random and draw each on the overlay with the left arm, right hand and tool: pull shrapnel from the forearm with pliers; dig out a bullet with a knife, then burn the wound with a lighter; pop a dislocated finger back (a fast sharp snap); rip a nail out of the thigh and bite your glove. The camera dips and rolls a little. You can still look. Enemies don't wait. Add blood on the glove afterward; it stays until you sleep.\n234\t- **Ampoule:** hold the syrette button .4 s at any time, or tap it during a fit (bottom of this section).\n235\t- **Hands on things:** repair (wrench on engine, 4 s), wheel at the Sill (both hands), jam clearing, reload. Draw hands for all of them.\n236\t\n237\t### Health\n238\t\n239\t100 HP, shown as 5 segments. A damaged segment refills on its own (6 HP/s after 6 s without damage) up to its top. Lost segments need a syrette or surgery. Carry at most 5 syrettes. No shields, no damage numbers.\n240\t\n241\t**At 0 HP:**\n242\t1. If a buddy is available (section 8), you go **down**: low camera, greyed veil, look only, then the rescue plays.\n243\t2. If not, a black card with one line of writing and two choices: reload last save, or quit to title.\n244\t\n245\t### The rasp (salt lung)\n246\t\n247\tThe sickness is the seabed dust. A hidden `lung` value climbs 0 to 100 in about 14 minutes of play. That rate is ×4 in a dust storm, ×6 within 15 m of burning cells, ×1.5 at a sprint.\n248\t\n249\t- **Warning (at 85):** you hear your breath catch. The experienced player holds the syrette button for an ampoule now, before it starts.\n250\t- **Fit (at 100):** 6 s. Harsh cough sounds, white bloom closing in (HUD veil), heavy view sway, walk ×.4. Noise alerts soldiers within 40 m, so a fit in the reeds beside a patrol is the worst moment in the game, as intended. An ampoule ends it at once and sets `lung` to 0.\n251\t- **No ampoule:** the fit lasts 14 s, then `lung` is 30. A second fit within 3 minutes blacks you out and counts as 0 HP: a buddy rescue, or the black card.\n252\t\n253\tAmpoules never appear in shops. You start with 2, hold at most 6, and get them only from Sister Perpetua in exchange for Clinic runs. The player always knows how many are left because the box is in the hand when used (draw that many glass tubes) and on the map's margin in pencil.\n254\t\n255\t---\n256\t\n257\t## 5. Weapons and wear\n258\t\n259\tMr. Sunday names his stock after church offices (\"my congregation\"). Numbers are starting points; all of them scale with Dev mode sliders. Enemies have 100 HP. Damage falls off from *full* to 70% at *half*, as in the kit's `fall`.\n260\t\n261\t| id | Name | Slot | Dmg | Rate/s | Mag / reserve | Auto | Wear/shot | Jam× | Full / half (m) | Stones | Notes |\n262\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n263\t| `verger` | Verger revolver | side | 34 | 3 | 6 / 36 | no | .004 | .5 | 30 / 60 | free | Head ×2. Your first gun, rusty. |\n264\t| `acolyte` | Acolyte flare pistol | side | 12 | .8 | 1 / 8 | no | .01 | .3 | 50 / 80 | 4 | Ignites where it lands; lights the night for 8 s. |\n265\t| `deacon` | Deacon SMG | primary | 16 | 13 | 32 / 224 | yes | .0025 | 1.2 | 20 / 45 | 5 | Cheap, jumpy, jams. |\n266\t| `parson` | Parson rifle | primary | 24 | 9.5 | 30 / 180 | yes | .002 | 1 | 60 / 140 | 8 | The kit's rifle. Honest. Remove the sci-fi holo; use iron sights. |\n267\t| `sexton` | Sexton pump | primary | 11×9 | 1.25 | 6 / 30 | no | .006 | .8 | 8 / 24 | 6 | The kit's shotgun. |\n268\t| `curate` | Curate bolt rifle | primary | 80 | .8 | 5 / 30 | no | .006 | .5 | 200 / 400 | 10 | Head ×2.5. Scope FOV 18. Sway. |\n269\t| `choir` | Choir LMG | heavy | 26 | 11 | 80 / 160 | yes | .0012 | 1.4 | 60 / 140 | 12 | Tracers: 2% per hit to ignite grass. |\n270\t| `bishop` | Bishop RPG | heavy | 200 | .3 | 1 / 5 | no | .03 | .6 | 300 | 14 | Blast 7 m. Ignites r = 4 m. Wrecks a jeep. |\n271\t| `gardener` | Gardener flamethrower | heavy | 40/s | hold | 100 / 200 fuel | yes | .0008 | .4 | 12 | 16 | Ignites each cell along the stream. Below 10% condition the tank may burst on you. |\n272\t| `machete` | Machete | melee | 60 | 1.6 | | | | | | free | The kit's punch and lunge, with a blade. Silent. |\n273\t| `bottle` | Bottle (molotov) | throw | | | carry 4 | | | | | 2 for 1 | Ignites r = 6 m. Draw the rag, flame and arc. |\n274\t| `frag` | Grenade | throw | 110 | | carry 4 | | | | | 2 for 1 | The kit's frag. r = 7 m. |\n275\t\n276\tYou carry one each of side, primary and heavy, plus machete and throwables. Every gun needs a first-person model and a body model in `drawGun`, with hands placed by `VM` entries.\n277\t\n278\t**Condition `cond` runs 1 to 0**, lowered by `wear` each shot. **Guns taken from enemies start at .2-.55. Guns from your safe-house crate start at 1.**\n279\t\n280\t- **Jam:** on each shot, if `cond < .7`, chance is `TUNE.jam * W.jam * .09 * (1 - cond / .7)^2`. A jam is a dead click and a stuck gun. □ or R clears it: a 1.1-1.6 s animation, one of three (slap the magazine, rack the bolt, bang it on your palm). The gun is silent until it's done.\n281\t- **Misfire:** below .15, a further 8% chance of a dud click (.4 s lost).\n282\t- **Break:** at `cond` 0 the gun splits. A short ugly animation, a bang and a curse, and you drop it. The slot is empty. Pick up whatever the dead left you.\n283\t- **You see the wear on the gun:** `drawGun` colours mix toward rust `#6a4a30` as `cond` falls, with tape and rag patches below .5, and the action sound gets rattlier. Below .4 a small 5-tick condition mark shows beside the ammo count. Enemies' guns jam too (5% of bursts): they stop, curse in gibberish and rack it, which teaches the mechanic and gives you a window.\n284\t\n285\t**Getting guns:** spend stones at Mr. Sunday's to unlock a gun (section 10). From then on every safe-house **weapon crate** hands you a fresh copy free, and refills your ammo, syrettes and throwables. That makes coming home matter.\n286\t\n287\t---\n288\t\n289\t## 6. Fire\n290\t\n291\tThis is the game's signature and it must feel dangerous. VERIFIED on WebGL 2 and WebGPU: a 512×512 cell grid over the map (4 m a cell), a burn-mask texture read by the terrain and grass shaders, 2,400 camera-facing flame quads, smoke columns. Spread simulation cost is trivial: 240 ticks with 6,000 burning cells took 61 ms. Code is in Appendix A4.\n292\t\n293\t**Cells:** `state` 0 unburnt, 1 burning, 2 ash, 3 wet; `t` time left burning. `fuel` is looked up lazily from `biomeAt` and cached: grass 1, scrub .7, reeds 2.5 (burns 6 s, tall flames), mud .25, trees (a cell with a trunk: burns 25 s, a tall column), and salt, rock, road, pad, brine 0. Zero fuel is a firebreak. Roads, the Causeway, the salt core and burnt ground all stop it. Fire can't cross those except by **embers**: each burning tree or reed cell has a small chance per tick to light a random cell up to 24 m downwind, so a firebreak is safe until it isn't.\n294\t\n295\t**Spread:** at 4 Hz, for each burning cell and each unburnt neighbour, `p = TUNE.fireSpread * .018 * fuel * exp(1.5 * wind * cos(angle to wind)) / dist`. With the default wind this measured about 2.5 m/s downwind on grass and almost nothing upwind. You can outrun it on foot on grass. In reeds it is faster than you.\n296\t\n297\t**The black is safe.** Ash doesn't burn. The player's first lesson (prologue) is to run through a thin flame line onto burnt ground rather than away from the fire. Back-burning (lighting a strip ahead of a fire so it has nothing left) works by itself in this model and is the key to contract C4. Water cells (flood) go to state 3 with white steam puffs.\n298\t\n299\t**What lights it:** Acolyte flares, Bottles, the Gardener, RPG and frag blasts (60% within r = 4 m), exploding fuel barrels (r = 8 m, and they chain), burning wrecks (one cell per 2 s), Choir tracers, kicked or shot campfires, and burning men running. Dev mode has \"ignite here\".\n300\t\n301\t**What it does:**\n302\t- **Actors in a burning cell:** take 25 HP/s, and catch fire for 3 s after leaving.\n303\t- **The player:** orange vignette, a roaring sound, lung ×6. The way out is a straight line through to ash.\n304\t- **Soldiers:** check `fireNear(x, y, 24 m)` at each think. If it's close they break off and flee to the nearest ash or zero-fuel cell, or across the wind. On fire, they run blind, screaming in gibberish, and light what they run through. They die in about 6 s.\n305\t- **Vehicles:** 6 HP/s; barrels, ammo crates and fuel dumps cook off after 2-4 s.\n306\t- **Sight:** line of sight through thick smoke (within a burning 32 m block) halves enemy sight range.\n307\t\n308\t**What it leaves:** terrain and grass go black through the mask (A4). Trees swap to charred materials on the next `streamScatter` pass (`mats: { l: '#191512 bark', b: '#191512 bark' }`), and a charred tree has no crown fuel. The mask is saved with the game. Ash regrows over two in-game days: the mask fades and fuel returns. The player's route across the country stays written on it for a while. That is the point.\n309\t\n310\t**Picture and sound:**\n311\t- Flames: 4 quads per cell within 70 m, 1 larger quad per cell out to 320 m.\n312\t- One `world.light` at the nearest large front, flickering. Embers from `game.particles`.\n313\t- Smoke rises with the wind. Fire looks best at dusk and night; don't blow it out by day (the flame gain in A4 is tuned).\n314\t- Crackle plus a low roar, by distance to the nearest burning cell and panned by bearing.\n315\t\n316\t---\n317\t\n318\t## 7. Vehicles\n319\t\n320\tUse `world.vehicle` (VERIFIED on the kit's terrain: a car reached its 420 u/s top speed, hit a wall and recovered, with or without a renderer). Draw your own models with the pen via `o.draw` (section 2.4 item 9). All of them are rusty, one-off, mismatched panels.\n321\t\n322\t| Type | kind | top | accel | turn | hp | Notes |\n323\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n324\t| Jeep | car | 420 | 300 | 2.1 | 120 | Open top. Clinic's is white with a hand-painted cross of no known church. |\n325\t| Technical | car (`r` 8.5, `mass` 2) | 360 | 240 | 1.9 | 180 | Pickup with a pintle gun in the bed. △ moves you to the gun when stopped. |\n326\t| Truck | car (`r` 10, `mass` 3) | 300 | 180 | 1.6 | 260 | Canvas back. Convoys. |\n327\t| Airboat | hover (`float` 7) | 380 | 240 | 2.3 | 100 | Fan cage. Fast on brine and reeds, a crawl elsewhere. |\n328\t\n329\t**Surfaces** (`surfaces` and `ground: (x, y) => biome tag`, both real engine options):\n330\t- Land vehicles: road full; grass `{top: .7}`; salt `{top: .95, grip: .6}`; mud `{top: .5}`; reeds `{top: .4}`.\n331\t- Airboat: brine and reeds full, mud `.5`, salt `.35`, everything else `.25`.\n332\t\n333\t**Seat:**\n334\t- On entering, set the hero non-solid and `place` it at the vehicle each step. The camera sits at the seat offset, turned by `v.angle` plus free look.\n335\t- The overlay draws steering wheel, hands (turning with `v.steer`), dash, and a **dash locator** with bearing and metres to the objective. Draw windscreen frame and bonnet in the world with the pen.\n336\t- On exit, place the hero at ground beside the door after a clear-ground ray.\n337\t\n338\t**Damage:**\n339\t- Crashes: `v.bumped` at speed costs `speed * .08`. Bullets cost 1 each, RPG 140, fire 6/s.\n340\t- Under 50%: white smoke. Under 20%: black smoke, a coughing engine, top speed halved, and it may stall.\n341\t- Repair: get out, stand at the bonnet, hold use: a 4 s wrench animation back to 60%. Unlimited, but it takes 4 s while someone is shooting.\n342\t- At 0: it catches, burns 3 s, explodes (r = 6 m), and leaves a burning wreck that lights grass.\n343\t\n344\t**AI vehicles:** convoys and patrol technicals follow the road polylines with look-ahead steering and `v.update(dt, null, { steer, gas })`. A technical's gunner fires bursts at the player within 120 m. A blocked or wrecked vehicle's crew get out and fight. Patrols run the roads about every 3 in-game hours, so driving is never just driving.\n345\t\n346\t**The radio** plays when you're in a vehicle and is the game's comic relief (section 9).\n347\t\n348\t---\n349\t\n350\t## 8. People\n351\t\n352\t### Factions\n353\t\n354\tBuild `LOOKS` entries from these. Bodies come from the kit's pen skeleton. Replace the helmets, visors and masks with a head function that draws: skin ball, small nose, two eye dots, brows, optional hair cap, beard, beret, scarf, bush hat, sunglasses, a nun's veil, and a jaw that moves with speech. Vary skin tone, scale (k .95-1.05) and head item by each soldier's seed.\n355\t\n356\t| Who | Colours | Head |\n357\t|---|---|---|\n358\t| **The Provisional Authority** (\"the Authority\", soldiers \"Provisionals\"): the old army, now the caretaker government. Clean paperwork, dirty hands. | cloth `#7a7350`, plate `#5d5a3e`, pack `#57523a` | Olive beret; officers wear peaked caps and sunglasses. |\n359\t| **The Front for the Return of the Waters** (\"the Front\"): rebels who became what they fought. | Mismatched cloth, red scarf `#9c2a1e`, vest `#3b2f26` | Red headscarf, bare heads, a few looted berets. |\n360\t| **Smugglers** at the Fleet | Oilskin `#4a4a3a`, rubber boots | Woollen caps. |\n361\t| **Civilians** | Dull robes and blankets, no plates or pouches (`plain: true`) | Bare or wrapped. Bundles. |\n362\t\n363\t### Soldier AI (extend `raiderStep`; keep its sight, hearing, bursts, strafing, hunt)\n364\t\n365\t- Remove shields. Add `faction`, and states `fleeFire`, `burning`, `wounded`.\n366\t- **Wounded:** 30% of non-head, non-explosive kills instead drop to a crawl (`b.move = 'crawl'`) at 14 u/s, firing a pistol weakly, shouting. A comrade within 15 m may drag him (`act 'drag'`): both stop shooting. He dies after 25 s, or sooner if you decide.\n367\t- **Squads:** with 3 or more fighting, one flanks (goal offset 40 m to the side), one suppresses your last known place.\n368\t- **Officers** radio for help after 15 s of combat. A technical comes from the nearest post (subtitled radio bark).\n369\t- **Night:** sight ×.55. Crouched in reeds: ×.5. Your fit's cough and unsilenced shots override this. Sight checks stay at the kit's rate.\n370\t- **Barks:** gibberish voice with a subtitle, only from the nearest soldier in 40 m, at most one every 3 s (section 9).\n371\t\n372\t### Guard posts and papers\n373\t\n374\tEach post: barrier, sandbags, 4-6 men, one mounted gun, a windsock, a fuel barrel someone left too close.\n375\t\n376\t- **Staying clear:** a cleared post stays clear until you next sleep. Then each cleared post has a `TUNE.postRespawn` (.4) chance to refill.\n377\t- **Papers:** while you hold a current contract from a faction, or the clinic's pass in P1, you carry its papers (shown as a slip in hand when challenged). That faction's posts wave you through, with a bark, unless you aim at them for more than 1 s, fire near them, or are in a stolen vehicle. Do any of that and your papers for that faction are **burned** until your next contract with them, and the whole post opens up. The other faction shoots on sight. Holding papers lets the player walk into a camp and start a fire at the worst moment. Encourage it.\n378\t\n379\t### Buddies\n380\t\n381\tAt New Game you choose one of four mercenaries from their passports (section 11). The other three exist in the world as **buddies**.\n382\t\n383\t| Merc | Who | As you, you get | As a buddy |\n384\t|---|---|---|---|\n385\t| **Teodor Lisk** | Sapper. Bulgarian. Says little, and it's usually about the blast radius. | Bottles ignite +2 m, start with 2 | Arrives with a Bottle already lit |\n386\t| **Nkechi Lund** | Field medic, Lagos by way of Malmö. Clipped, unsentimental. | Surgery 25% faster, carry 7 syrettes | Rescue restores 80 HP, not 60 |\n387\t| **Ruy Almeida** | River pilot from Lisbon. Won't stop joking; it's how you know he's scared. | Repair in half the time | Arrives in a vehicle and leaves it for you |\n388\t| **Hollis Tran** | Crop-duster and courier from the Gulf Coast. Bitter. Keeps count of who owes her. | Shotgun wear halved | Stays and fights 60 s longer |\n389\t\n390\tOrder the three: **Best Buddy** (meet at the Salt Shed in P1), **Second** (at the Lamp, when you first reach it), **Third** (at the Tannery). They sit at their safe house with a drink and small talk between jobs.\n391\t\n392\t**Rescue (they save you):** at 0 HP or blackout, if a living buddy is free, `TUNE.rescueChance` (.8) applies, with a 2-minute cooldown.\n393\t1. The buddy spawns out of sight 40-60 m away and runs in, shooting (6-9 s).\n394\t2. You watch it from the ground, unable to act, and hear them coming.\n395\t3. They grab your harness and drag you 6 m. The camera jolts and the ground slides by.\n396\t4. They stab you with a syrette (visible hand in your face) and say a line. You're up at 60 HP.\n397\t5. They fight beside you until the area's clear or 60 s pass, then leave. If it fails (they die getting to you), it's the black card.\n398\t\n399\t**Rescue (you save them):** a buddy who drops sits against something, bleeding, calling for you by name (a bark and a locator ring). You have 45 s to reach them. Hold use and choose:\n400\t- **Syrette**, if you have one. Uses it; they're up.\n401\t- **Mercy** (your sidearm, one shot, your hand in shot). In a burning cell or below 5 s left, it's the only option.\n402\t\n403\tDeath is permanent and saved. Their bottle stays on the table at their safe house. Later lines change to fit: Sister counts them; Sunday asks about the debt.\n404\t\n405\t**Alt-plans:** after you accept a contract, the locator rings. A buddy offers a side-plan (two big prompts; you keep moving). Accepting adds a richer objective and a nasty written twist, pays more, and adds 1 to their `rep` (`rep` raises their rescue priority and their line quality). Twists are in the contract table. Every alt-plan goes wrong the way it's written to.\n406\t\n407\t**Civilians.** Crowds at Saint Odile's, the Salt Shed camp and Port Clement. They do nothing clever: walk, sit, run from fire and gunfire. If you kill one, Sister Perpetua knows (\"Four hundred and seven. Then you came.\"), gives one fewer ampoule next time, and says it.\n408\t\n409\t**Animals (Stretch):** a herd of 8 pale antelope on the south-west grass that bolts from gunshots and fire, and vultures that circle over bodies. Quadrupeds are your own pen drawing: four two-bone legs on a sine.\n410\t\n411\t---\n412\t\n413\t## 9. Story and writing\n414\t\n415\t### Tone (TASK.md: a writing style like the source's)\n416\t\n417\tWeary, dry, literary, cruel in passing.\n418\t- People talk as if they've had this conversation before.\n419\t- Short sentences. War is paperwork, arithmetic or weather, never glory.\n420\t- Nobody explains themselves. Nobody calls you a hero. No exclamation marks except in screams."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ea1b61d-e1b8-4350-be76-ae4a4cbc51a9","uuid":"29beb999-6f50-4e0f-a3c0-f6c1c77840d6","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:14:06.144Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211923__xa49253/work/PLAN.md","content":"# PLAN: an original open-world mercenary game inspired by Far Cry 2\n\nYou build this alone, from this plan and TASK.md. Nobody can answer questions, so where the plan is silent, decide and move on.\n\n- **Read before coding:** sections 1 and 2, and Appendix A. Read the rest when your milestone (section 15) reaches it.\n- **Paths** are relative to your dev folder (the subfolder TASK.md tells you to create). \"The deliverable\" is the single HTML file TASK.md names, one level above the dev folder.\n- **The title is yours.** Follow TASK.md's title rule (brainstorm, then pick with a real random number generator). Nothing in this plan depends on the title. This plan just says \"the game\".\n- **VERIFIED** means the director ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04, on engine v0.88.0 (commit `0a46310`), on WebGL 2, and on WebGPU where stated. Trust those and spend your effort elsewhere.\n- **Everything is required unless marked (Stretch).** Section 15 says what to cut if you must. Whatever you cut, cut it whole: TASK.md allows no stubs, and no line of dialogue may point at something that isn't there.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** You are a contractor with a ruined lung, sent into a burned-out republic on the shore of a dead inland sea to kill the old engineer who drained it. Two armies pay you to betray each other. Everything you touch wears out: your rifle, your truck, your lungs, your friends. Fire is your best weapon and your worst neighbour. At the end you are standing at a dam with your hands on a wheel, and you have to decide who gets to drown.\n\n**Genre:** open-world first-person shooter with survival pressure (guns that fail, a sickness, friends who can die for good).\n\n**Perspective:** first person, always. Your eyes never leave your head: no cutscene cameras, no third-person toggle, no fade-to-talk. Conversations happen while you stand there and can look around. Why: the source's identity lives in the hands and the eyes, and the engine ships a 2 km first-person open-world kit that already moves, shoots and streams well.\n\n**The five pillars. Every feature must serve one.**\n1. **Fire is the weather of war.** Dry grass, wind and one spark redraw the map. Burn scars stay.\n2. **Everything wears out.** Guns jam and split, trucks smoke and stall, lungs fail, friends die. You repair, replace and ration.\n3. **Your hands are the interface.** Map, locator, monocular, syrette and pliers are objects you hold, and the world keeps moving while you hold them. No minimap, no floating markers, almost no HUD.\n4. **Friends are your save system and your guilt.** Buddies drag you out of fights. You have to drag them out too, or end it for them. They stay dead. At the end they sell you.\n5. **No clean hands.** Both sides are the same, and every contract makes things worse. People talk about it as if it were paperwork.\n\n**Taken from the source (mechanics and mood only):** spreading grass fire used as a weapon; weapon decay and jamming; a recurring sickness managed with rationed medicine; a real-time map in the hand; first-person field surgery; rescuing buddies with permanent death; choosing one of several mercenaries; two interchangeable factions; uncut diamonds as money with a beeping locator; safe houses with a bed and a time-skip; guard posts on the roads; a charismatic villain you came to kill; bleak, literary, weary writing.\n\n**Must be ours (TASK.md: an original game, not a recreation).** All names, characters, story, map, levels, art, music and sounds in this plan are original. Do not use the source's proper nouns or lookalikes: its country and towns, its factions (APR, UFLL), the Jackal, its characters (Reuben, Josip, Nasreen, Marty, Frank, Hakim, Paul and the rest), its opening scene, its tapes, its ending, its logo or its music. Do not set the game in a real country or name a real army. If in doubt, invent.\n\n**Where we beat the source.** It is famous for three annoyances. Do not repeat them:\n- **Guard posts that refill the moment you drive past.** Ours stay cleared until you sleep, and they may not refill even then.\n- **Everyone shooting you on sight.** Ours check your papers (section 8).\n- **Malaria on a dumb timer.** Our sickness responds to what you do: smoke and dust bring it on, and you can feel it coming (section 4).\n\n**Length:** 60 to 90 minutes for a first run through the whole story, with every feature used at least once. A second player can finish faster.\n\n**Three moments the systems must be able to produce without a script.** If these can happen, the game is working:\n- You're crouched in reeds at night with a patrol 30 m away and your lung at 90. You have one ampoule. You hear your breath catch.\n- You put two bursts into a man, your rifle clicks dead, and while you rack it the grass behind him goes up, and the wind is coming your way.\n- You're on your back in the dirt, and through the smoke you see your friend running toward you. You owe them for the last time, too.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Foundation\n\n### 2.1 Get the pieces\n\n```sh\n# in the dev folder\ngit clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git engine-repo\ngit -C engine-repo fetch --depth 1 origin 0a463102661e66de714aa76b321219f20028ab04\ngit -C engine-repo checkout -q FETCH_HEAD        # v0.88.0: what this plan was verified on\nmkdir -p engine hub src test\ncp engine-repo/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine-repo/engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js engine/\ncp -r engine-repo/vendor/three-0.182.0 engine-repo/vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3 engine/\ncp engine-repo/src/starter3d/00-shell.js engine-repo/src/starter3d/30-shooter.js engine-repo/src/starter3d/90-start.js src/\nfor f in session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js; do curl -fsSO --output-dir hub https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n```\n\nThe engine files in `engine/` are yours to patch. Keep a one-line log of every patch in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`: TASK.md asks for it in Engine Feedback. Delete `engine-repo/` when you finish.\n\n**The stack.** The handoff package's 2D engine still runs the game loop, scenes, input, menus, settings, sound and the HUD. `E.World3D` (three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3) draws and simulates the 3D world under it. This is the route the handoff README's step 7 names for first person. Do not build on the 2D views, and do not write your own renderer.\n\n**What to read in `engine-repo/`, and nothing more:**\n- `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` lines 1-264 (the whole 3D manual).\n- `dist/handoff/API.md`: the Game, Input, Dialog, Menu, Sound and Standard features sections.\n- `dist/handoff/docs/ASSETS.md`: when you write props.\n- Grep `dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md` for names; never read it whole.\n- `docs/ENGINE-3D.md`, section \"Things to know\".\n- `node dist/handoff/tools/asset-find.mjs \"tent\"` searches the CC0 prop store (trees, tents, crates, furniture). There are no modern vehicles or weapons in it: you draw those.\n\n### 2.2 Build into one file (VERIFIED)\n\nAn esbuild bundle of the kit booted from `file://` in about 7 s with no errors and no server. The file came to about 4.8 MB before any game code, with three.js and Rapier inlined, so it needs no CDN and no network. The hub's importer leaves embedded engines alone. Appendix A1 has the build script and page template.\n\n- Keep game code as numbered files in `src/`, concatenated in order into one ES module. Remove the kit's `const SHOOT = (() => {` wrapper, so everything lives at module top level.\n- Suggested files: `00-shell`, `05-data` (tables only), `06-story` (text only), `10-land`, `20-soldier`, `30-player`, `40-weapons`, `45-fire`, `50-vehicles`, `55-ai`, `60-buddies`, `65-contracts`, `70-hud`, `75-screens`, `80-audio`, `85-net`, `88-bot`, `90-start`. `src/metas.html` holds the hub tags (section 13); `src/page.css` holds the CSS in A7.\n- **Data lives in tables.** TASK.md wants the rest of the game buildable later with normal designer tools. So sites, roads, weapons, looks, vehicles, contracts, dialogue, barks, radio and the shop are plain data in `05-data` and `06-story`. Systems read those tables. Adding a contract or a site must not touch a system.\n\n### 2.3 The kit, section by section\n\n`src/30-shooter.js` is 2,088 lines and 200 KB. Read a section when you change it, not all of it at once. Line numbers are for v0.88.0.\n\n| Kit section | Lines | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| TUNING | 20-24 | Keep walk/run/eye constants. Delete shields, slide, zipline, wall climb. |\n| THE BADLANDS: `baseH`, `height`, `color`, `OUTPOSTS`, `ROADS`, `route`, `planLand` | 28-146 | Keep `route`, the rasters and `planLand`. Rewrite `baseH`, `color` and the data (section 3). |\n| Structure pieces: `quad`, `boxAx`, `cylAx`, `quadPts`, `B`, `beam`, `CYL`, `wall`, `shack`, `container`, `ladder`, `tower`, `tank` | 147-308 | Keep. Add `hull`, `lighthouse`, `dam`, `quay`, `tentRow`. Delete `zipline`. |\n| `layout()` | 310-400 | Rewrite, one function per site. |\n| `structureMesh` | 416-436 | Keep (one merged mesh, one material; kinds pick patterns). Add a rust kind if you want one. |\n| SCATTER: `chunkItems`, `streamScatter` | 437-488 | New tree kinds, charred variants (section 6). |\n| GRASS: `grassMeshes`, `buildGrass`, `drawGrass` | 489-547 | Add the burn mask (A4) and tall reeds. |\n| FX: tracers, decals, `impactStar`, `drawFlashes` | 548-647 | Keep. |\n| THE SOLDIER: `LOOKS`, `body`, `pose`, `deadPose`, `drawSoldier`, `drawGun` | 648-1015 | Add looks, heads, faces, new guns, crawl/burn/drag poses (section 8). Remove the sci-fi visors and masks. |\n| WEAPONS: `WEAPONS`, `reloadCurve`, `fire`, `impact`, `explode` | 1016-1152 | New table, wear and jam (section 5). Remove shields, damage numbers and the loud hit-marker kinds. |\n| THE PLAYER: `newState`, `look`, `move`, `actions`, `camStep` | 1153-1465 | Remove slide, climb, zip and the third-person view. Keep vault, mantle, ladders. Add tools, health, lung, vehicle seat. |\n| RAIDERS: `spawnRaider`, `streamRaiders`, `raiderStep`, `raiderShoot`, `hurtPlayer` | 1466-1598 | Base for all soldier AI (section 8). |\n| WORKING PARTS: doors, bins, loot | 1599-1695 | Keep doors. Bins become crates. |\n| FIRST PERSON: `drawViewModel`, `keysAt`, `VM` | 1696-1792 | Add every new gun and every hand animation (section 4). |\n| HUD | 1793-1885 | Rewrite to almost nothing (section 11). Delete the compass bar, outpost counter and control list. |\n| SOUNDS | 1886-1919 | Base table; extend (section 12). |\n| THE STEP: `step(dt, I)`, `keepPrev`, `onDraw`, `reset`, `run` | 1920-2052 | **Keep this seam.** `step(dt, I)` takes an input object `I = { move, mouse, stick2, pressed(a, w), down(a), use(a) }`. Real input, the bot, attract mode and tests all drive the game through `I`. `run(n)` is the replay proof: keep one and test it. |\n| `touchLook`, `TOUCH`, `touchButtons` | 1928, 2053+ | Delete. TASK.md wants no touch controls. |\n\nFrom `00-shell.js` keep the `game` creation and helpers; delete the slice menu, `FamilyMenu` and title stage. `90-start.js` becomes your boot (section 13 and Appendix A6). Use **one world for everything**: the title screen and attract mode are that same world with a fixed camera. This saves boot time, which matters in section 13.\n\n### 2.4 Engine facts that will otherwise cost you hours\n\n1. **Units:** 16 units = 1 m. x east, y south, z up. Your own three.js meshes are in metres with y up: `E.World3D.toThree(x, y, z)`, or x/16, z/16, y/16.\n2. **Fixed steps:** `update` runs at 1/120 s. A frame past 50 ms gives up its time, so under software rendering (about 4.5 fps here) game time crawls. Tests must wait on state, never on the clock.\n3. **Make every mesh and material during boot, before `world.prepare()`.** A material first seen mid-game stalls. `prepare()` warms visible world meshes and all overlay meshes, even hidden ones. A world-scene pool mesh must be visible with a draw range of at least one triangle at that moment (the kit's trick: `setDrawRange(0, 3)`).\n4. **Renderer rules:** node materials and TSL only. No `ShaderMaterial`, `onBeforeCompile`, `EffectComposer`, addons, compute or GPU read-back. An `InstancedMesh` over 1,000 instances must not use `DynamicDrawUsage`. For a dynamic pool, use a merged `BufferGeometry` with `setDrawRange`, as the kit's tracers and the flames in A4 do. A texture read in a vertex stage needs `.level(0)`.\n5. **The pen** (`world.pen`, `overlay.pen`) draws for one frame. Call it each frame from `onDraw`. Draw changes nothing in the sim.\n6. **Randomness:** the sim uses `E.rng(seed)` only, so `world.hash()` replays. `Math.random` is for pictures. In a shared-world session the host's sim uses its own `AIHub.rng(label)` stream.\n7. **`game.go` fades for .22 s.** Set `game.fadeTime = 0` in tests.\n8. **World-private fields used by recipes in Appendix A:** `world._sun`, `_sunDir`, `_ambient`, `_skyDome`, `_fog`, `terrain.mat`. They exist at the pinned commit, which is why you pin.\n9. **`vehicleParts` is engine code** with three fixed kinds. Patch one line so your own models draw: `if (a.o.draw) a.o.draw(this.pen, a, t); else vehicleParts(this.pen, a, t);` at `my-3d2dge-3d.js` line 3006.\n10. **`E.Menu` does not scroll** at v0.88.0. Keep lists to 6 rows or page them.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The world\n\n**The Republic of Lacustria**, a country named for a lake that isn't there. It is a high basin ringed by red rock. The **Amaranth**, an inland sea, was drained over forty years by a river diversion for cotton. Rusting trawlers sit on the dry bed, forty kilometres from any water. A lighthouse stands in a field. Toxic salt dust blows off the seabed. A ceasefire was signed in the spring, and nobody has stopped shooting.\n\nKeep the kit's 2,048 m square map (`SIZE = 32768`). Crossing it takes about 80 s by jeep, which is right for a slice with this many stops.\n\n### Sites (metres east, metres south)\n\nPut these in `SITES`. `planLand` nudges each to flat ground and flattens a pad of radius `r`.\n\n| id | Name | at | r | Held by | What stands there |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `strip` | Vasco Strip | 1500, 1640 | 50 | nobody | Gravel runway, wrecked crop-duster, windsock. The prologue starts near here. |\n| `mission` | Saint Odile's | 420, 1520 | 46 | clinic | Roofless chapel, 12 tents, water tank, the clinic jeep. Sister Perpetua. |\n| `salt` | the Salt Shed | 1000, 1130 | 30 | safe house | Long tin shed, white salt heaps, on the Causeway. Refugee camp (30 tents) 120 m south. |\n| `fleet` | the Fleet | 1180, 1290 | 90 | smugglers | Nine stranded trawler hulks, tilted, some you can walk inside. |\n| `remnant` | the Remnant | 1120, 1400 | 130 | nobody | The last brine, knee deep. Flat water. The airboat lives here. |\n| `reedcamp` | Reed Camp | 760, 1400 | 40 | Front | Boardwalks, tents, fuel barrels, in the tallest reeds. |\n| `port` | Port Clement | 1640, 1010 | 80 | neutral, then ash | Quay wall facing nothing, 14 buildings, the Customs House (3 floors), the Harbourmaster bar, Mr. Sunday's boat in dry dock. |\n| `k9` | Kilometre Nine | 1530, 600 | 50 | Authority HQ | Sandbags, containers, watch tower, flag, Captain Dray's office. |\n| `red` | Red Steps | 430, 850 | 50 | Front HQ | Quarry terraces cut into a red mesa, one tunnel, watch tower, a schoolroom with a chalkboard. |\n| `st4` | Station Four | 900, 720 | 44 | Front | Pump house, pipes, tank, a dry canal cut. |\n| `lamp` | the Lamp | 1060, 660 | 26 | safe house | A 40 m lighthouse on a stone spur above the old north shore. Its light turns at night. You can navigate by it from anywhere. |\n| `tannery` | the Tannery | 440, 1180 | 28 | safe house | Vats, hide racks, a shack, on the old west shore. |\n| `watertower` | the Water Tower | 1380, 1600 | 22 | safe house | Tank on legs, shack under it. |\n| `sill` | the Sill | 1000, 190 | 60 | Authority + Meridian | A dam wall across a canyon notch in the rim. Control house, helipad, dark reservoir behind. |\n\n**Roads** (`ROADS`, routed by the kit's A*):\n- port-k9, k9-lamp, lamp-st4, st4-red, red-tannery, tannery-mission\n- mission-watertower, watertower-strip, strip-port\n- **the Causeway:** tannery-salt, salt-fleet (spur), salt-port, a raised road 2 m above the seabed running east-west\n- lamp-sill, up the canyon\n\n**Guard posts** (`POSTS`: `{ on: [a, b], at: .5, faction }`):\n- G1 port-k9 Authority\n- G2 k9-lamp Authority\n- G3 tannery-mission Front\n- G4 st4-red Front\n- G5 salt-port Authority\n- G6 watertower-strip Front\n\n### Terrain (rewrite `baseH` and `color`)\n\n- **Bowl:** let `d = hypot((m-1050)/600, (n-1180)/430) + (fbm(m/300, n/300, 3) - .5) * .35` and `sea = 1 - sstep(.92, 1.08, d)`. Multiply the kit's relief by `(1 - sea)` and add `-14 * sea` metres, so the seabed is low and nearly flat.\n- **Mesas:** keep the kit's two-tier mesas, but only where `sea == 0`, weighted toward the west and north-west (Red Steps).\n- **Rim:** keep the kit's cliff ring. Cut a notch 280 m wide at `m ≈ 1000` on the north edge for the Sill's canyon.\n- **Remnant:** make the terrain perfectly flat at one height inside its circle. There is no swimming. Draw a water plane a hand above it (A5). Walkers wade at 60%. The airboat is happy.\n\n`biomeAt(m, n)` returns one of: `salt` (seabed core, cracked, near white), `mud` (seabed rim, sparse sedge), `reeds` (a broad band inside the old shore in the south-west, from about (700, 1250) to (1000, 1560)), `grass` (default upland), `scrub`, `rock` (steep), `road`, `pad`, `brine`. The fire, vehicle surfaces, grass mesh and held map all read it.\n\n**Palette:** bleached gold grass `#c6aa64`, olive scrub `#8e8a55`, salt `#d9d2bd`, cracked mud `#a9967a`, reed `#b9b277`, rust `#7a3a1e`, oxide hull `#4f5c50`, ash `#191512`.\n\n**Scatter** (`chunkItems`):\n- Flat-topped thorn trees: the `tree` shape with a low wide crown.\n- `bottle` trees: your own `E.SHAPES` entry, a fat `lathe` trunk with a tiny crown. These are the silhouette the player remembers.\n- Dead snags and rocks.\n- Nothing on salt except wreckage: nets, anchors, ribs of small boats.\n\n**Structures** are merged into the kit's one mesh over `world.block` colliders, using its pieces plus yours:\n- `hull(P, ...)`: 28 m long, 7 m beam, tilted 6-14°, plates as quads, rust patches, a hole in the side, an open hold.\n- `lighthouse`, `quay`, `tentRow`.\n- `dam`: wall 240 m wide and 46 m tall, three gates, a service stair, a top walkway, a control house with a big iron wheel, a separate reservoir water plane behind.\n\n**Art direction.** The engine's native look: low-poly pen-drawn bodies with outlines, pixel font, chunky textures. Keep the 3D picture at `balanced`: the kit already passes `res: 'balanced'`, and A7 makes the Settings row agree. It looks good at 1080p and still reads as this engine. Never smooth it.\n\n### Time, wind, weather\n\n- **Time of day:** 1 in-game hour = 90 s (slider). Use A3, whose keyframes were tuned on screenshots. Night must be dark but playable: moonlit blue, with fire and the Lamp as the bright things. Update the sun's direction every 5 game minutes, not every frame, or shadows shimmer.\n- **Wind:** `windDir` drifts slowly and `wind` runs 0.2 to 1. Every 2 in-game hours a new target eases in over a minute. Show it only in the world: grass lean (drive the kit's sway from it), smoke drift (A4), windsocks at posts and strips, and a tiny pencil arrow on the map's compass rose.\n- **Dust storm:** about one per game day, 2-3 minutes. Fog pulls in to about 120 m and turns orange (`world._fog.near/far`, `scene.fog.color`), sand particles, wind 1, lung ×4. Enemy sight halves for everybody. Fire runs fast. Tell the player in advance by radio.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The player and the hands\n\n### Controls (prompts always come from `game.input.label(action)`)\n\n| Action | DualSense | Keyboard and mouse |\n|---|---|---|\n| Move / look | L stick / R stick | WASD / mouse (captured on click) |\n| Fire / aim | R2 / L2 | Left click / right click |\n| Jump, vault, mantle | ✕ | Space |\n| Crouch | ○ | C or Ctrl |\n| Sprint | L3 | Shift |\n| Use (tap); reload or clear jam when nothing to use | □ | E use; R reload or clear jam |\n| Next weapon | △ | Q; 1, 2, 3 direct |\n| Melee (machete) | R3 | V |\n| Bottle / frag | L1 / R1 | F / G |\n| Map / locator / monocular / syrette | D-pad up / right / left / down | M / N / B / H |\n| Pause | Options | Esc |\n| **In a vehicle:** gas / brake, reverse | R2 / L2 (analog: `input.value`) | W / S |\n| Steer / look | L stick / R stick | A, D / mouse |\n| Handbrake / exit | ✕ / □ | Space / E |\n| Gun seat (technical) / radio | △ / L1 | Tab / L |\n\nUse one `KEYS` map for every scene. Bind only `Mouse0` plus the pad code for fire and aim. VERIFIED: `label` then gives `Left click`, or `R2` on a pad. The kit's extra `KeyJ` is what made it print \"J\". With a pad and glyphs `playstation`, VERIFIED labels are `R2 L2 ✕ □ △ ○`. With `xbox` they are `RT` and `A X`. Name every action in `menu.controls` so the Controls screen reads well.\n\n### Moving\n\nKeep the kit's walk 68, run 118, crouch 40, ADS 46 units/s, its vault, mantle and ladders. Sprint runs on stamina: 8 s to empty, 12 s to refill. Lower the jump a little. Kick doors with melee. Grounded, heavy, tired.\n\n### Holding things (pillar 3)\n\nEach tool is a stance on the overlay pen, keyframed with the kit's `keysAt`. Raising one takes .3-.45 s with a sound. The world never pauses. **Fire drops any tool at once.**\n\n- **Map (A2):** both hands, the gun gone. Walk at 60%. It shows contour lines, roads, site names, pencil circles on the current objective, your tagged soldiers as pencil ticks, a small arrow for you, a compass rose with the wind arrow. Your left wrist is in shot with a **watch**: the only clock in the game. Look input moves a pencil over the map instead of the camera. ✕ or left click pins a waypoint. L2/R2 or the wheel zoom between the whole basin and 3×. The crosshair and prompts hide. You can raise it while driving, and you will crash. That's the point.\n- **Locator:** a chunky handset in the left hand, gun still in the right (hip fire only, spread ×2). A ring of 8 LEDs points to the pinned waypoint or current objective, with a small readout in metres (A2's method on a 128×64 canvas). Within 150 m of an unfound diamond case it beeps, from every 2 s down to every .15 s at 5 m, and a separate red LED blinks with it. It also rings for radio calls.\n- **Monocular:** FOV 70 to 14, dark tube mask, slight sway. Held .6 s on a soldier, vehicle, ammo crate or fuel dump, it tags the thing: a pencil tick on the map and a small corner bracket seen only through the monocular. Tags clear when the site is clear.\n- **Syrette (tap):** .9 s jab in the thigh, +40 HP.\n- **Field surgery:** when health is in the last segment, the syrette button instead starts a 4-6 s operation you can't cancel. It restores you to 60 HP and costs no syrette. Pick one of four at random and draw each on the overlay with the left arm, right hand and tool: pull shrapnel from the forearm with pliers; dig out a bullet with a knife, then burn the wound with a lighter; pop a dislocated finger back (a fast sharp snap); rip a nail out of the thigh and bite your glove. The camera dips and rolls a little. You can still look. Enemies don't wait. Add blood on the glove afterward; it stays until you sleep.\n- **Ampoule:** hold the syrette button .4 s at any time, or tap it during a fit (bottom of this section).\n- **Hands on things:** repair (wrench on engine, 4 s), wheel at the Sill (both hands), jam clearing, reload. Draw hands for all of them.\n\n### Health\n\n100 HP, shown as 5 segments. A damaged segment refills on its own (6 HP/s after 6 s without damage) up to its top. Lost segments need a syrette or surgery. Carry at most 5 syrettes. No shields, no damage numbers.\n\n**At 0 HP:**\n1. If a buddy is available (section 8), you go **down**: low camera, greyed veil, look only, then the rescue plays.\n2. If not, a black card with one line of writing and two choices: reload last save, or quit to title.\n\n### The rasp (salt lung)\n\nThe sickness is the seabed dust. A hidden `lung` value climbs 0 to 100 in about 14 minutes of play. That rate is ×4 in a dust storm, ×6 within 15 m of burning cells, ×1.5 at a sprint.\n\n- **Warning (at 85):** you hear your breath catch. The experienced player holds the syrette button for an ampoule now, before it starts.\n- **Fit (at 100):** 6 s. Harsh cough sounds, white bloom closing in (HUD veil), heavy view sway, walk ×.4. Noise alerts soldiers within 40 m, so a fit in the reeds beside a patrol is the worst moment in the game, as intended. An ampoule ends it at once and sets `lung` to 0.\n- **No ampoule:** the fit lasts 14 s, then `lung` is 30. A second fit within 3 minutes blacks you out and counts as 0 HP: a buddy rescue, or the black card.\n\nAmpoules never appear in shops. You start with 2, hold at most 6, and get them only from Sister Perpetua in exchange for Clinic runs. The player always knows how many are left because the box is in the hand when used (draw that many glass tubes) and on the map's margin in pencil.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Weapons and wear\n\nMr. Sunday names his stock after church offices (\"my congregation\"). Numbers are starting points; all of them scale with Dev mode sliders. Enemies have 100 HP. Damage falls off from *full* to 70% at *half*, as in the kit's `fall`.\n\n| id | Name | Slot | Dmg | Rate/s | Mag / reserve | Auto | Wear/shot | Jam× | Full / half (m) | Stones | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `verger` | Verger revolver | side | 34 | 3 | 6 / 36 | no | .004 | .5 | 30 / 60 | free | Head ×2. Your first gun, rusty. |\n| `acolyte` | Acolyte flare pistol | side | 12 | .8 | 1 / 8 | no | .01 | .3 | 50 / 80 | 4 | Ignites where it lands; lights the night for 8 s. |\n| `deacon` | Deacon SMG | primary | 16 | 13 | 32 / 224 | yes | .0025 | 1.2 | 20 / 45 | 5 | Cheap, jumpy, jams. |\n| `parson` | Parson rifle | primary | 24 | 9.5 | 30 / 180 | yes | .002 | 1 | 60 / 140 | 8 | The kit's rifle. Honest. Remove the sci-fi holo; use iron sights. |\n| `sexton` | Sexton pump | primary | 11×9 | 1.25 | 6 / 30 | no | .006 | .8 | 8 / 24 | 6 | The kit's shotgun. |\n| `curate` | Curate bolt rifle | primary | 80 | .8 | 5 / 30 | no | .006 | .5 | 200 / 400 | 10 | Head ×2.5. Scope FOV 18. Sway. |\n| `choir` | Choir LMG | heavy | 26 | 11 | 80 / 160 | yes | .0012 | 1.4 | 60 / 140 | 12 | Tracers: 2% per hit to ignite grass. |\n| `bishop` | Bishop RPG | heavy | 200 | .3 | 1 / 5 | no | .03 | .6 | 300 | 14 | Blast 7 m. Ignites r = 4 m. Wrecks a jeep. |\n| `gardener` | Gardener flamethrower | heavy | 40/s | hold | 100 / 200 fuel | yes | .0008 | .4 | 12 | 16 | Ignites each cell along the stream. Below 10% condition the tank may burst on you. |\n| `machete` | Machete | melee | 60 | 1.6 | | | | | | free | The kit's punch and lunge, with a blade. Silent. |\n| `bottle` | Bottle (molotov) | throw | | | carry 4 | | | | | 2 for 1 | Ignites r = 6 m. Draw the rag, flame and arc. |\n| `frag` | Grenade | throw | 110 | | carry 4 | | | | | 2 for 1 | The kit's frag. r = 7 m. |\n\nYou carry one each of side, primary and heavy, plus machete and throwables. Every gun needs a first-person model and a body model in `drawGun`, with hands placed by `VM` entries.\n\n**Condition `cond` runs 1 to 0**, lowered by `wear` each shot. **Guns taken from enemies start at .2-.55. Guns from your safe-house crate start at 1.**\n\n- **Jam:** on each shot, if `cond < .7`, chance is `TUNE.jam * W.jam * .09 * (1 - cond / .7)^2`. A jam is a dead click and a stuck gun. □ or R clears it: a 1.1-1.6 s animation, one of three (slap the magazine, rack the bolt, bang it on your palm). The gun is silent until it's done.\n- **Misfire:** below .15, a further 8% chance of a dud click (.4 s lost).\n- **Break:** at `cond` 0 the gun splits. A short ugly animation, a bang and a curse, and you drop it. The slot is empty. Pick up whatever the dead left you.\n- **You see the wear on the gun:** `drawGun` colours mix toward rust `#6a4a30` as `cond` falls, with tape and rag patches below .5, and the action sound gets rattlier. Below .4 a small 5-tick condition mark shows beside the ammo count. Enemies' guns jam too (5% of bursts): they stop, curse in gibberish and rack it, which teaches the mechanic and gives you a window.\n\n**Getting guns:** spend stones at Mr. Sunday's to unlock a gun (section 10). From then on every safe-house **weapon crate** hands you a fresh copy free, and refills your ammo, syrettes and throwables. That makes coming home matter.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Fire\n\nThis is the game's signature and it must feel dangerous. VERIFIED on WebGL 2 and WebGPU: a 512×512 cell grid over the map (4 m a cell), a burn-mask texture read by the terrain and grass shaders, 2,400 camera-facing flame quads, smoke columns. Spread simulation cost is trivial: 240 ticks with 6,000 burning cells took 61 ms. Code is in Appendix A4.\n\n**Cells:** `state` 0 unburnt, 1 burning, 2 ash, 3 wet; `t` time left burning. `fuel` is looked up lazily from `biomeAt` and cached: grass 1, scrub .7, reeds 2.5 (burns 6 s, tall flames), mud .25, trees (a cell with a trunk: burns 25 s, a tall column), and salt, rock, road, pad, brine 0. Zero fuel is a firebreak. Roads, the Causeway, the salt core and burnt ground all stop it. Fire can't cross those except by **embers**: each burning tree or reed cell has a small chance per tick to light a random cell up to 24 m downwind, so a firebreak is safe until it isn't.\n\n**Spread:** at 4 Hz, for each burning cell and each unburnt neighbour, `p = TUNE.fireSpread * .018 * fuel * exp(1.5 * wind * cos(angle to wind)) / dist`. With the default wind this measured about 2.5 m/s downwind on grass and almost nothing upwind. You can outrun it on foot on grass. In reeds it is faster than you.\n\n**The black is safe.** Ash doesn't burn. The player's first lesson (prologue) is to run through a thin flame line onto burnt ground rather than away from the fire. Back-burning (lighting a strip ahead of a fire so it has nothing left) works by itself in this model and is the key to contract C4. Water cells (flood) go to state 3 with white steam puffs.\n\n**What lights it:** Acolyte flares, Bottles, the Gardener, RPG and frag blasts (60% within r = 4 m), exploding fuel barrels (r = 8 m, and they chain), burning wrecks (one cell per 2 s), Choir tracers, kicked or shot campfires, and burning men running. Dev mode has \"ignite here\".\n\n**What it does:**\n- **Actors in a burning cell:** take 25 HP/s, and catch fire for 3 s after leaving.\n- **The player:** orange vignette, a roaring sound, lung ×6. The way out is a straight line through to ash.\n- **Soldiers:** check `fireNear(x, y, 24 m)` at each think. If it's close they break off and flee to the nearest ash or zero-fuel cell, or across the wind. On fire, they run blind, screaming in gibberish, and light what they run through. They die in about 6 s.\n- **Vehicles:** 6 HP/s; barrels, ammo crates and fuel dumps cook off after 2-4 s.\n- **Sight:** line of sight through thick smoke (within a burning 32 m block) halves enemy sight range.\n\n**What it leaves:** terrain and grass go black through the mask (A4). Trees swap to charred materials on the next `streamScatter` pass (`mats: { l: '#191512 bark', b: '#191512 bark' }`), and a charred tree has no crown fuel. The mask is saved with the game. Ash regrows over two in-game days: the mask fades and fuel returns. The player's route across the country stays written on it for a while. That is the point.\n\n**Picture and sound:**\n- Flames: 4 quads per cell within 70 m, 1 larger quad per cell out to 320 m.\n- One `world.light` at the nearest large front, flickering. Embers from `game.particles`.\n- Smoke rises with the wind. Fire looks best at dusk and night; don't blow it out by day (the flame gain in A4 is tuned).\n- Crackle plus a low roar, by distance to the nearest burning cell and panned by bearing.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Vehicles\n\nUse `world.vehicle` (VERIFIED on the kit's terrain: a car reached its 420 u/s top speed, hit a wall and recovered, with or without a renderer). Draw your own models with the pen via `o.draw` (section 2.4 item 9). All of them are rusty, one-off, mismatched panels.\n\n| Type | kind | top | accel | turn | hp | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Jeep | car | 420 | 300 | 2.1 | 120 | Open top. Clinic's is white with a hand-painted cross of no known church. |\n| Technical | car (`r` 8.5, `mass` 2) | 360 | 240 | 1.9 | 180 | Pickup with a pintle gun in the bed. △ moves you to the gun when stopped. |\n| Truck | car (`r` 10, `mass` 3) | 300 | 180 | 1.6 | 260 | Canvas back. Convoys. |\n| Airboat | hover (`float` 7) | 380 | 240 | 2.3 | 100 | Fan cage. Fast on brine and reeds, a crawl elsewhere. |\n\n**Surfaces** (`surfaces` and `ground: (x, y) => biome tag`, both real engine options):\n- Land vehicles: road full; grass `{top: .7}`; salt `{top: .95, grip: .6}`; mud `{top: .5}`; reeds `{top: .4}`.\n- Airboat: brine and reeds full, mud `.5`, salt `.35`, everything else `.25`.\n\n**Seat:**\n- On entering, set the hero non-solid and `place` it at the vehicle each step. The camera sits at the seat offset, turned by `v.angle` plus free look.\n- The overlay draws steering wheel, hands (turning with `v.steer`), dash, and a **dash locator** with bearing and metres to the objective. Draw windscreen frame and bonnet in the world with the pen.\n- On exit, place the hero at ground beside the door after a clear-ground ray.\n\n**Damage:**\n- Crashes: `v.bumped` at speed costs `speed * .08`. Bullets cost 1 each, RPG 140, fire 6/s.\n- Under 50%: white smoke. Under 20%: black smoke, a coughing engine, top speed halved, and it may stall.\n- Repair: get out, stand at the bonnet, hold use: a 4 s wrench animation back to 60%. Unlimited, but it takes 4 s while someone is shooting.\n- At 0: it catches, burns 3 s, explodes (r = 6 m), and leaves a burning wreck that lights grass.\n\n**AI vehicles:** convoys and patrol technicals follow the road polylines with look-ahead steering and `v.update(dt, null, { steer, gas })`. A technical's gunner fires bursts at the player within 120 m. A blocked or wrecked vehicle's crew get out and fight. Patrols run the roads about every 3 in-game hours, so driving is never just driving.\n\n**The radio** plays when you're in a vehicle and is the game's comic relief (section 9).\n\n---\n\n## 8. People\n\n### Factions\n\nBuild `LOOKS` entries from these. Bodies come from the kit's pen skeleton. Replace the helmets, visors and masks with a head function that draws: skin ball, small nose, two eye dots, brows, optional hair cap, beard, beret, scarf, bush hat, sunglasses, a nun's veil, and a jaw that moves with speech. Vary skin tone, scale (k .95-1.05) and head item by each soldier's seed.\n\n| Who | Colours | Head |\n|---|---|---|\n| **The Provisional Authority** (\"the Authority\", soldiers \"Provisionals\"): the old army, now the caretaker government. Clean paperwork, dirty hands. | cloth `#7a7350`, plate `#5d5a3e`, pack `#57523a` | Olive beret; officers wear peaked caps and sunglasses. |\n| **The Front for the Return of the Waters** (\"the Front\"): rebels who became what they fought. | Mismatched cloth, red scarf `#9c2a1e`, vest `#3b2f26` | Red headscarf, bare heads, a few looted berets. |\n| **Smugglers** at the Fleet | Oilskin `#4a4a3a`, rubber boots | Woollen caps. |\n| **Civilians** | Dull robes and blankets, no plates or pouches (`plain: true`) | Bare or wrapped. Bundles. |\n\n### Soldier AI (extend `raiderStep`; keep its sight, hearing, bursts, strafing, hunt)\n\n- Remove shields. Add `faction`, and states `fleeFire`, `burning`, `wounded`.\n- **Wounded:** 30% of non-head, non-explosive kills instead drop to a crawl (`b.move = 'crawl'`) at 14 u/s, firing a pistol weakly, shouting. A comrade within 15 m may drag him (`act 'drag'`): both stop shooting. He dies after 25 s, or sooner if you decide.\n- **Squads:** with 3 or more fighting, one flanks (goal offset 40 m to the side), one suppresses your last known place.\n- **Officers** radio for help after 15 s of combat. A technical comes from the nearest post (subtitled radio bark).\n- **Night:** sight ×.55. Crouched in reeds: ×.5. Your fit's cough and unsilenced shots override this. Sight checks stay at the kit's rate.\n- **Barks:** gibberish voice with a subtitle, only from the nearest soldier in 40 m, at most one every 3 s (section 9).\n\n### Guard posts and papers\n\nEach post: barrier, sandbags, 4-6 men, one mounted gun, a windsock, a fuel barrel someone left too close.\n\n- **Staying clear:** a cleared post stays clear until you next sleep. Then each cleared post has a `TUNE.postRespawn` (.4) chance to refill.\n- **Papers:** while you hold a current contract from a faction, or the clinic's pass in P1, you carry its papers (shown as a slip in hand when challenged). That faction's posts wave you through, with a bark, unless you aim at them for more than 1 s, fire near them, or are in a stolen vehicle. Do any of that and your papers for that faction are **burned** until your next contract with them, and the whole post opens up. The other faction shoots on sight. Holding papers lets the player walk into a camp and start a fire at the worst moment. Encourage it.\n\n### Buddies\n\nAt New Game you choose one of four mercenaries from their passports (section 11). The other three exist in the world as **buddies**.\n\n| Merc | Who | As you, you get | As a buddy |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Teodor Lisk** | Sapper. Bulgarian. Says little, and it's usually about the blast radius. | Bottles ignite +2 m, start with 2 | Arrives with a Bottle already lit |\n| **Nkechi Lund** | Field medic, Lagos by way of Malmö. Clipped, unsentimental. | Surgery 25% faster, carry 7 syrettes | Rescue restores 80 HP, not 60 |\n| **Ruy Almeida** | River pilot from Lisbon. Won't stop joking; it's how you know he's scared. | Repair in half the time | Arrives in a vehicle and leaves it for you |\n| **Hollis Tran** | Crop-duster and courier from the Gulf Coast. Bitter. Keeps count of who owes her. | Shotgun wear halved | Stays and fights 60 s longer |\n\nOrder the three: **Best Buddy** (meet at the Salt Shed in P1), **Second** (at the Lamp, when you first reach it), **Third** (at the Tannery). They sit at their safe house with a drink and small talk between jobs.\n\n**Rescue (they save you):** at 0 HP or blackout, if a living buddy is free, `TUNE.rescueChance` (.8) applies, with a 2-minute cooldown.\n1. The buddy spawns out of sight 40-60 m away and runs in, shooting (6-9 s).\n2. You watch it from the ground, unable to act, and hear them coming.\n3. They grab your harness and drag you 6 m. The camera jolts and the ground slides by.\n4. They stab you with a syrette (visible hand in your face) and say a line. You're up at 60 HP.\n5. They fight beside you until the area's clear or 60 s pass, then leave. If it fails (they die getting to you), it's the black card.\n\n**Rescue (you save them):** a buddy who drops sits against something, bleeding, calling for you by name (a bark and a locator ring). You have 45 s to reach them. Hold use and choose:\n- **Syrette**, if you have one. Uses it; they're up.\n- **Mercy** (your sidearm, one shot, your hand in shot). In a burning cell or below 5 s left, it's the only option.\n\nDeath is permanent and saved. Their bottle stays on the table at their safe house. Later lines change to fit: Sister counts them; Sunday asks about the debt.\n\n**Alt-plans:** after you accept a contract, the locator rings. A buddy offers a side-plan (two big prompts; you keep moving). Accepting adds a richer objective and a nasty written twist, pays more, and adds 1 to their `rep` (`rep` raises their rescue priority and their line quality). Twists are in the contract table. Every alt-plan goes wrong the way it's written to.\n\n**Civilians.** Crowds at Saint Odile's, the Salt Shed camp and Port Clement. They do nothing clever: walk, sit, run from fire and gunfire. If you kill one, Sister Perpetua knows (\"Four hundred and seven. Then you came.\"), gives one fewer ampoule next time, and says it.\n\n**Animals (Stretch):** a herd of 8 pale antelope on the south-west grass that bolts from gunshots and fire, and vultures that circle over bodies. Quadrupeds are your own pen drawing: four two-bone legs on a sine.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Story and writing\n\n### Tone (TASK.md: a writing style like the source's)\n\nWeary, dry, literary, cruel in passing.\n- People talk as if they've had this conversation before.\n- Short sentences. War is paperwork, arithmetic or weather, never glory.\n- Nobody explains themselves. Nobody calls you a hero. No exclamation marks except in screams.","numLines":420,"startLine":1,"totalLines":952}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WStNN5ynUsGkpUxQxMDUza","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
